# Book 7 completion pass — Step 2: whole-arc read

Reader: Codex, independent fresh-context seat. Date: 2026-10-11.

Read before the manuscript: `EDITION_BRIEF.md`; all of `OWNER-DECISIONS.md`, with special attention to #18, #23–#25, #33, #42 and #46–#51; Book 7's `BOOK_MAP.md`, all eight AFTER MOVEMENT blocks and binding coordinator rulings in `STATE_LEDGER.md`, `protected-patterns.txt`, and `COMPLETION-PLAN.md`; Book 6 ch59–60; Book 8's opening plan; and Book 6's whole-arc read as the report model. Then read `manuscript/chapter-01.md` through `chapter-60.md` in order, every word, keeping `state/completion/whole-arc-notes.md` during the read. Mechanical audits followed: calendar, deployments, money, board, sheets, injuries, geography, quotations, knowledge releases, protected text, repetition, formula metrics, and the required source probe. No manuscript file was modified and no git command was run.

## Verdict

**PASS — RATIFY.** The book holds as a complete novel. It begins by making freedom from institutional measurement feel materially true: the board records deeds, not rank, and Cael can write `I'm free` without irony (ch7:27–103, :269–295). It then makes that freedom morally insufficient. Quiet ground turns his old absence into unique power (ch27:181–239; ch29:243–309), the Ring lets him become the only effective practitioner in the party (ch31:211–289; ch32:139–301), and he uses that advantage to take from Oryn without consent (ch36:65–225; ch37:141–240). The second half does not absolve him. It builds a rule through refusal, explicit permission, repayment and restraint (ch38:103–229; ch47:97–129; ch48:7–105; ch55:221–287; ch56:153–205). The ending therefore earns both claims at once: nobody out here can reduce him to an instrument, and the place where only he works still requires other people, their boundaries and their evidence (ch58:219–229; ch60:31–41, :247–265).

The calendar, deployment count, money, injuries, site geometry, names, knowledge limits, protected wording, Reader Standard and both book seams hold. Movements 3 and 7 earn their excess over the planning guide. The Hall adds a new opponent rule and a new cooperative solution rather than proving the Ring again (ch53:229–339; ch54:91–107, :191–323). The source-distance audit finds no surviving unprotected source-shaped passage. No line-level correction is warranted. The only recommended work is a controlled two-lane same-author texture pass: the book's hand/count/accounting motifs are appropriate, but its retrospective glosses, measured pauses and repeated evaluative looks make the surface more patterned than the events require.

## 1. Continuity

### Calendar, season and units — holds

The required dates are exact: shelf day 2 (ch1:3–7); Lowmarch day 9 (ch2:3); ford day 53 (ch9:317; ch10:3–9); mountain days 70–84 (ch15:1–7; ch22:323–331); Fallow days 94–96 (ch27:157–183; ch28:67–105; ch30:237); long reading day 100 (ch36:233–279; ch38:77–87); birthday day 102 (ch40:3–5, :189–209); Oryn north day 111 (ch44:225–261; ch45:3–15); Millrace day 113 (ch47:3–25, :121–153); Stair days 114–115 (ch49:3–19; ch52:3–21, :355–359); Hall day 119 (ch54:3–15; ch55:153–165); first snow day 129 (ch59:121–139; ch60:31); last ferry day 130 (ch60:47–95); Oryn's letter day 131 (ch60:219–225).

Manuscript-wide search found no season name, calendar month name or order, English weekday name, or metric unit. Generic `season` and `month` occur only in allowed senses (ch1:269; ch33:93; ch60:31). `Fathom by fathom` is a non-metric rope measure at ch60:91, not a violation.

### Deployments and fragment rules — holds

Every deployment log is monotonic: none through day 52 (ch1:3; ch9:317); one after the ford through the Fallow approach (ch11:419; ch27:239); thirteen after the Ring (ch33:223; ch46:195); fourteen after the first consented Tide read (ch48:281); twenty-three after the Stair (ch51:217; ch53:389); twenty-six after the Hall (ch55:163); and twenty-seven after the second consented Tide read, including the closing inventory (ch55:301; ch60:31). Practice, involuntary Path use and non-field tests are explicitly kept outside Cael's deployment definition (ch1:269; ch19:257–277; ch29:243–309; ch39:245–249).

The fragment mechanics do not drift. Iron reads air or body, never both (ch4:97); Storm lanes bill ten, ten and twelve and clear at eleven, eleven and thirteen (ch4:97; ch32:215; ch51:47); Wind locks the landing for one count (ch4:85; ch32:163; ch51:21–33); Anchor is sure at ten feet, faint at twelve and absent at fourteen (ch4:99; ch50:261–293); Compression acts at contact (ch4:91; ch51:51–59); Ember remains one point (ch32:47; ch51:73–83, :213); Blade remains partial (ch4:101); Tide remains self-and-contact and governed by the body's owner (ch38:39; ch48:17–23, :73–105; ch60:37).

Shadow is used exactly twice, at the Ring and Stair (ch32:117–127; ch50:213–255). Both uses are outside hostile sight; Oryn sees neither and never learns Seln is the source (ch34:25–47; ch51:165–173). The closing inventory states the same sealed logic without expanding it (ch60:37).

### Money, board, bolts and sheets — holds

The Lowmarch sale becomes a sealed reserve that never enters the working purse (ch3:101–137; ch7:269–279). Working cash proceeds coherently through 52 after the ford jobs (ch14:337–451), 116 after Oxhollow (ch21:345–361; ch22:323–329), 108 after the cart debt (ch24:233–251), 102 after the birthday room and kid (ch40:23–37, :383–389), and 96 after the cold-term paper room (ch60:3–13, :29–31). The Stair's 300 stays `taken; not paid` and never enters the purse (ch59:161–175).

The crew reaches the wall thirteenth, then tenth, then eleventh; Pike's final book has nine lines, leaves the rating blank, and the cloth still says eleventh (ch7:183–229; ch25:3–49; ch45:105–165; ch59:175–217; ch60:29–31). Brom recovers five bolts from the Ring while the sixth remains on its floor (ch34:113–151; ch35:15–23). The sheet count closes at eleven Ring, twenty-nine Stair and twelve Hall, fifty-two total (ch57:13–17; ch60:19–25).

### Bodies — holds

Lira's right ankle never mends; it remains taped and flat-footed through first snow (ch11:297–311; ch35:79–81; ch43:137–143; ch58:17–27; ch59:23–45). Oryn mends the tibia, not the ankle (ch20:205–263; ch21:155–207). The left forearm remains in its sling and is not silently released when snow comes (ch33:91–119; ch42:79–125; ch58:17–27).

Cael's calf reopens on mountain and Ring and closes by day 99; his ribs are retaped, later billed by the Hall, cold-aggravated, and quiet by day 129 (ch19:151–159; ch20:27–35; ch34:149–177; ch35:265–271; ch54:139–175; ch55:89–103; ch59:19–21, :47–49). Brom's one hand has four finger-pad cuts, his right shoulder takes the bolt and then the Hall reopening, and every treatment carries forward (ch34:113–151; ch49:237–273; ch51:125–153; ch54:139–175; ch55:25–45; ch56:33–67). Karis's hand and throat remain impaired until their staged recoveries; her mountain emptying is reserve exhaustion, not an untracked wound (ch19:125–173; ch20:41–53; ch34:113–137; ch35:241–251). Oryn's reserve is repeatedly priced and is full again only after three days without mending (ch34:179–225; ch47:43–85; ch51:125–153; ch60:109–119).

### Distances and geography — holds

Thornwater's gate-to-stone distance is 110 yards and the follower keeps forty-five yards (ch6:9, :107–137; ch5:189–201). The Ring has a twenty-six-yard-stride floor, eleven banded uprights, its den beneath the eastern lip, a sixty-two-cord-and-twenty-stride perimeter, and the western line stone 220 yards from the middle (ch27:165–167; ch28:189–237; ch30:57–61, :121–145, :177–181, :211–217). The apparently different 207 yards is measured from the crossed floor's western edge to the stone and therefore agrees with the thirteen-yard radius (ch32:3–9).

The Stair is 220 yards inside its line, forty steps down, with five courses per wall and the lone radius line between courses (ch49:3–5, :159–193; ch52:61–101, :291–311). The Hall lies on the recovered bearing and adds a third perimeter of the same size (ch52:177–239; ch53:3–35, :95–157; ch57:57–79). The fourth site is paced only from outside, is twice the radius, and lies on the continued bearing; the ending's four-site line is therefore supported without claiming identical interiors (ch58:117–229; ch60:237–265).

### Quotations, names and seams — hold

The first and second poles are exact at ch7:289 and ch44:219. `Don't declare anything` appears in its original form at ch5:169. Oryn's river-in-the-dark rule is intact at ch22:73. The covenant occupies ch36:201–225 and its final distinction is exact at ch36:217. Oryn's route condition is intact at ch47:201. The Ring and Stair `Later` exchanges match exactly at ch33:257–261 and ch50:205–209. Pike's documents and spoken ledger lines remain exact at their mapped positions (ch3:253–261; ch7:27–47; ch24:119–179; ch45:105–165; ch59:161–207).

No protected unnamed role gains a name: Teague's people remain spear/bow/boy/healer; Thornwater's household, the carters, the six on the rim and the fourth site remain unnamed (ch7:301–345; ch22:159–173; ch49:21–83; ch56:19–25; ch58:117–139). Spoken and written fragment lists use `the Bronze from Ardenmere` rather than Feryn beside Oryn (ch38:23–25; ch60:37).

The Book 6 seam is exact: second camp, first fire, three boxes and Seln's unopened case, with no Velmere letter carried east (ch1:3–7, :36–41, :168–183). The exit supplies Book 7's own required state—four aligned sites, one unopened and unnamed; Oryn's return condition; Teague after the thaw; 96 in hand; 52 sheets; 27 deployments; home at Lowmarch—without depending on Book 8's unreconciled plan (ch59:219–225; ch60:3–41, :215–265).

## 2. Knowledge boundaries and reserved truths

The stillhound's sensing rule is unknown until the evidence is assembled and tested at the ford: Seln's seven/dry-foot observation, the survivor, Karis's two-part hypothesis, the planned test, and the proof arrive in that order (ch8:285–301; ch9:75–115, :181–225, :229–317; ch10:55–107, :135–169; ch11:241–251, :375–399). Quiet ground remains carter rumor until the Fallow trials (ch12:303–357; ch27:181–229). `The Quieting` is not coined until Karis's day-103 grammar work (ch41:163–203, :237–291).

The book uses `[UNBOUND]` once, and only as a dating limit (ch42:269). `Compact` is spoken in the authorized stake discussion and later protected/book-ending contexts, not as early public knowledge (ch34:247–249; ch58:229; ch60:83). Oryn learns the fragment mechanism from Cael's telling, with sources identified by Path or protected description; no outsider tells her (ch38:3–47). Teague is neither read nor told; his wrist remains his information (ch56:153–205).

Karis's hypothesis is acknowledged and never stated, including in her private exchange with Cael (ch43:57–111; ch44:61). `The still place` remains Cael's private label until Oryn is told, and the crew hears it only afterward (ch55:193–203, :243–279; ch57:191–211). It is not presented as newly discovered at the ending (ch60:39). Nothing explains why the fragments answer inside a Quieting; the strongest statement remains `I don't know why` (ch38:25–31; ch60:261–265).

Outside the protected pending log wording, the prose does not assert an awake personal Path for Cael. The permitted maker statements stay at their mapped altitude: a circle is made (ch28:211), somebody made a floor that turns Paths off (ch43:93), someone made the aligned pattern and somebody drew the line before the registry (ch60:251–265). Seln's case is never opened; the survey index and stroke-and-bar remain undescribed in meaning; the fourth site is never entered or named (ch52:139–203; ch58:117–229).

## 3. The consent thread

The thread meets the Reader Standard without qualification. Before the taking, Cael states that Oryn cannot consent, admits that his real motive for silence is fear of her refusal, hears four people distinguish permission to read him from permission to take from her, and writes that he has chosen her cost (ch36:65–225). In the act, he knows that stopping is the right road and reaches anyway (ch37:141–171). The text neither excuses the choice nor disguises it as a misunderstanding.

Oryn then names the wrong precisely: he took her asking, reversed the terms of her table, and cannot appoint his own punishment or forgiveness (ch38:103–229). His new rule is not merely `ask`; Oryn adds purpose, forbidding him to use a willing body just to see whether he can (ch39:281–291; ch44:145–213).

Millrace tests the rule twice. He asks to read the boy, receives `no`, and steps back (ch47:97–129). He later asks Oryn a different question; she emphasizes that the wrist is hers and the boy's was not hers to grant, gives an explicit yes, and controls the depth and stopping point (ch48:7–105). No sentence attaches forgiveness to that permission.

The debt is paid through three self-readings, telling Oryn first and whole, and asking again before the second outward read (ch55:169–287). Payment is not absolution: Oryn's later viewpoint says she has not forgiven him (ch60:121–125). At Teague's wrist Cael neither reads nor asks, because curiosity is not a sufficient purpose (ch56:153–205). A thirteen-year-old is left in no doubt that the taking was wrong, Cael knew it, Oryn set the terms, and the body-owner rule now governs him. No sentence reads as endorsement, retroactive consent or forgiveness.

## 4. Book-wide repetition — the worst ten

Counts are case-insensitive occurrence pools across the sixty manuscript files. They identify what a human texture pass should inspect, not words to delete indiscriminately.

1. **Hands — 1,508 occurrences (687 in ch1–30; 821 in ch31–60).** Hands are the book's moral and practical image—work, reading, healing, taking, restraint—but they become the default stage business too (ch1:39–67; ch33:91–119; ch48:17–23; ch56:153–205). Preserve bodily-consent and craft uses; thin neutral placement and handling.
2. **Count-family — 608 (279/329).** Counting is character, mechanics and ethics, but almost every kind of attention becomes a count (ch1:5–49; ch32:163–215; ch47:145–153; ch59:191–199). The last third risks making tactical, medical and emotional attention sound alike.
3. **`That was` explanatory codas — 334 (155/179).** The narration often gives an image or statement and then interprets it through `That was...` (ch1:63; ch15:57–71; ch38:223–225; ch55:179). This is the most audible new sentence move across movement boundaries.
4. **Retrospective `as [person] had...` comparisons — 315 (125/190).** Present action is repeatedly pulled back through a prior scene (ch1:123–241; ch33:165; ch54:91–107; ch60:69–83). The callbacks aid continuity but, by the Hall, make new action feel pre-glossed.
5. **Accounting lexicon — enter-family 112, price-family 105, cost/bill-family 147.** The ledger language is the novel's design, yet it spreads beyond logs into ordinary thought and gesture (ch4:91–109, :183; ch34:23; ch46:259–285; ch55:275–287). Keep actual logs, contracts and costs; thin metaphorical bookkeeping where the scene already shows the price.
6. **`Which was` — 115 (46/69).** This clause often adds an immediate interpretive gloss after a concrete fact (ch1:29, :199; ch40:53; ch55:179). It is unobtrusive locally and conspicuous in aggregate.
7. **`As if / as though` — 90 (52/38).** Many comparisons clarify unfamiliar work, but the same hinge also explains faces and silences that could stand unaided (ch2:149–183; ch37:33–61; ch50:293).
8. **`At once` — 89 (43/46).** It is used for recognition, simultaneous Path response, sudden light and immediate speech (ch1:237–247; ch3:229; ch40:123; ch51:41). The physical uses are clear; the speech/realization uses can be varied.
9. **Measured pauses — 89 (42/47).** `For a moment/while`, `after a while`, `for some time`, and breath/count-length pauses give nearly every silence a ruler (ch2:27–29; ch3:91–163; ch35:335; ch59:193). The form suits this book but becomes predictable.
10. **Repeated evaluative looks — 69 (44/25).** Characters look once, twice or again to verify a person, document or count (ch2:149; ch3:253–275; ch11:321–327; ch60:57–69). Pike owns this as a signature; outside Pike and deliberate verification it is over-shared.

The Book 6 watch list is much improved. Exact `squared` occurs twice; quarter-turns do not occur; `for a moment/while` plus `after a while` occurs eleven times; `did not look` four; `nobody said` three; `did not ask` eight; `He found that` four; and the approximate `not X but Y` pool is nineteen. Exact `flat` appears 111 times, but the great majority are literal terrain, a flat-footed gait, a flat hand or site geometry rather than the old voice tic (ch1:77–109; ch23:133–163; ch29:155–165). `Let it stand` occurs eight times and `put/set it by` eight; those are smaller but audible shared habits (ch10:285; ch45:165; ch55:179; ch60:255).

## 5. Arc and pacing

### The argument

M1 makes the board a genuine alternative measure: the ledger says what the crew did and corrects itself in public (ch7:27–103). M2 tests that freedom against bodies: Lira loses the reliable floor of measurement, Brom discovers work without a Path still pays, and Seln's unasked-for reports remain his (ch13:159–205, :255–321). M3 converts those claims into craft on the mountain—repeatable measurement, freely offered reports, and trained bodies—then makes the accident collect its price (ch16:131–217; ch18:91–123; ch19:33–173).

M4 turns `nobody reads me` into a dangerous exception. Five Arbiters stop together; Cael's fragments do not; he conceals that fact from the group (ch27:181–239; ch29:243–309). M5 gives the exception its triumph and moral failure in the same movement: it saves the party at the Ring and enables the non-consensual taking from Oryn (ch31:211–289; ch32:139–301; ch36:65–225; ch37:141–240). M6 converts guilt into a rule and evidence into restraint rather than theory (ch38:103–229; ch41:43–95; ch43:57–111; ch44:145–219). M7 tests the rule under refusal and consent, then weaponizes the Quieting at the Stair (ch47:97–129; ch48:7–105; ch49:21–121; ch50:143–295). M8 makes the exceptional power cooperative rather than solitary and closes on orientation, not explanation (ch54:191–323; ch55:221–287; ch60:247–265).

### Length and local pacing

