   309	Karis held it for a count of six. He counted it for her. At six the wall went down all at once, and she sat back against Seln's knees with her hands open in her lap.
   310	
   311	It was full dark. The stars were out. He lay where he had fallen and went over it, since somebody had to.
   312	
   313	Two were dead: one on a white floor, with a bolt in its eye, and one a yard from his boots. Seven had gone, north and south, and nobody on that hill could have said more of them than that. Six people were on the western side of a grey stone. Four of the six were hurt, and he would have the count of that by lamplight. He knew already that every hurt but his own had been taken by somebody who had no means of answering. Nine things lay under his hand again, and they had not yet stopped shaking.
   314	
   315	There were no instruments, and there would be no hundred marks.
   316	
   317	He could say what had been done, as Pike would have said it. A thing loud enough to be hunted had been made in the one place where nothing could be loud. It had taken a pack off five people who could not make a sound that the pack would hear, and a line of fire had kept it from following them out. That was the whole of the win.
   318	
   319	The pack had learned nothing from it that it could use. It had never needed to learn anything, and he did not suppose that it would begin now.
   320	
   321	And five people had watched him go up a hill with fire in his fist. Four of them had seen one thing more than the fifth had.
   322	
   323	Nobody spoke to him. Somebody was striking a flint.

