    67	He lay between two sacks with Hesk's book under his hand. Lira had brought it up the ladder at dusk inside her coat, and with it one sentence, said exactly as a healer had said it: that it was a true page, and that true was not the same as paid. He had written nothing in it since.
    68	
    69	In the dark, with nothing else to attend to, the listening had the whole of him.
    70	
    71	His own current went along at the edge of every thought, quick and slow. He had had it since the trough, and he had begun to know its ways. It quickened when he thought of a table. It dragged when he was tired. And somewhere toward the middle of the night he noticed that it was not one sound, and that it never had been; he had only wanted quiet enough to tell.
    72	
    73	They rose out of it singly, as the furniture of a room comes up when the eyes grow used to the dark.
    74	
    75	Wind was the first, since Lira was asleep six feet from him and it had always leaned her way. It was a dry hiss, like the pale grass of the Fallow going over in a breath of air, and it lived in his feet. The read came after, low and even, under his soles: a millstone turning, heard through a floor. Something ticked in his right hand, as a lamp-wick ticks when the oil is poor, and he knew whose fire that was; and under his breastbone Pressure drew and filled, slowly, like a bellows worked by a tired arm that has a long day's iron still in front of it.
    76	
