   120	
   121	The grain came on the hundred and first morning, in three carts from the river country, and by the middle of the forenoon it was going up the ladder past the place where they slept.
   122	
   123	It was the last of the year's. The carters had come up through the stretch at noon the day before and slept in the cart-yard, three heavy men in sheepskins, with frost on their mules' eyelashes; and they wished to be unloaded and gone before the roads grew any harder. Holds farther up the valley would fetch that barley through the whole of the cold term, a cart at a time. Until then it lay in Thornwater's loft, under Thornwater's thatch, which was all that a grain hold was for.
   124	
   125	It was also how the five paid for their bread there. Nobody had struck a bargain. The steading's woman had looked at Brom's shoulders on the first evening, and at the height of her loft, and had said that her own men were two short; and Brom had said that he would be glad of something to lift.
   126	
   127	So Brom took two sacks at a time, one on each shoulder, and went up the rungs like a man going upstairs to bed. Seln took one, and made no sound with it. Karis sat on an upturned keg at the ladder's foot with the hold's slate on her knee, and cut a stroke for every sack that passed her. Lira had been given nothing, since there was nothing that a person could do with a sack and one arm. She sat on the well's coping with her foot flat on the stones and counted the strokes on Karis's slate, aloud, to annoy her.
   128	
   129	Cael carried one at a time. It was the first load that he had had on his back since a mountain, and the ribs took it without a word. He went up past his own blanket at each turn, and past Hesk's satchel on its nail, and set the sack down on a heap that had begun level with his knee and now stood as high as his chest; and by the tenth it had come to him that he was being walled into his own bedroom with other people's breakfast.
   130	
