   134	
   135	It had made room. There was a new thing under his breastbone, as light as a wren, and the heaviest of the ten had moved over for it as a great dog moves over on a hearth.
   136	
   137	He had been afraid of that weight since the day it came to him, and had lain awake more than once over what it might do if it ever rolled when he was not ready. He stood at a well with a dipper in his hand and heard it settle, and all that he felt was a kind of respect.
   138	
   139	He hung the dipper on its nail, and stepped away from the rim, and the ground was not where he had left it.
   140	
   141	It was there. His boot came down on the frozen earth of the yard as it had come down all morning. But he felt it come down half a beat after it had done so, as a man hears an axe from across a valley after he has watched it fall.
   142	
   143	He knew that lag, for he had run up a ditch in it with eight hounds behind him. Whenever the heaviest thing in him had been used, everything else answered him a beat late for ten counts or twelve, and he had entered that in Hesk's book as a bill and learned to wait it out.
   144	
   145	He counted to twelve. He took a stride, and the ground arrived late under it. He counted twelve more, and it was no better.
   146	
   147	He had laid no lane, and so this was not a bill. The thing had moved house, and it meant to be late until it had done unpacking.
