   286	
   287	He had known since a loft what it was to reach for somebody who had not been asked. He had done it the morning before, on her own table, with his eyes open.
   288	
   289	"I ask first. Every time."
   290	
   291	"Don't say it to please me. Say it because you were the one who didn't, once, and you've seen what it's like from where I stand." She turned back toward her bench. "I've four more before dark, and I'm six days short at this gate."
   292	
   293	He carried eleven sacks more before the light went, on feet that told him of each rung a little after he had climbed it, and he did not fall off the ladder.
