   154	
   155	There was light enough from the frost. He opened the book on the well's rim, at the leaf where an inventory ended, and wetted the pencil, and wrote it under her eyes without hurrying. He had written a leaf once in a loft so that he could never claim to have been surprised, and it had permitted nothing; he wrote this one so that there should be a line in his own hand that he could be held to.
   156	
   157	*The reading turned outward. I ask first. Every time. If I ever don't, that's the day I became the instrument.*
   158	
   159	She read it upside down, with her lips.
