   223	---
   224	
   225	Oryn went out by the northern wicket as the hundred and eleventh day was breaking, and for the first quarter of a mile she kept putting her hand out for a cheek-strap.
   226	
   227	It was not there, and she had known that it would not be. She had walked that track thirty times with a small brown head at her right shoulder, and her hand had thirty rounds of habit in it; it went out at every turn of the path, and closed on air, and came back. She let it. A person who has set a great many bones does not expect a habit to knit in a morning.
   228	
   229	It was a hard grey dawn, with the frost standing an inch deep on the grazing, and her own breath went up in front of her at the pace that she walked. Behind her a hold was waking. She heard the sweep of the well creak, and a goose, and the bar being drawn at the western gate for five people and three boxes.
   230	
   231	The steading's woman had been at the wicket with a loaf and a cold fowl in a cloth.
   232	
   233	"It's yours when you're here," she had said, of a back room, as she said it every round. And then, which she had never said before: "You were here a good while."
   234	
   235	"Sixteen days too long."
   236	
   237	"I'd not have said too long."
   238	
   239	That was all. The woman had shut the wicket behind her.
   240	
   241	The track went up through the hold's grazing, past a stone hut, toward a low place in the hill. The goats lifted their heads to see her go by with nothing on a rope. She had a pannier on her own back, with the strap across her chest, and it rode where no pack had ridden since her first year: tape, flask, shears, linen, a cup, a needle, and a loaf. She had the spare rope over her shoulder. It was thirty feet of good three-strand, and she had laid it up herself; and she had carried it for eleven rounds without once needing it.
   242	
   243	She would need it tomorrow night. There was a jenny at the next gate that was the worst-led beast on seven roads.
   244	
   245	She did her sum as she climbed. She did it every morning, as another person might say her name over.
   246	
   247	Out of that gate on the hundred and eleventh, when she had said the ninety-fifth: sixteen. Two days of road to the next. Two gates after it, and an old woman on the way whose knee had never once kept to a healer's days; that was where the odd one would go. Then the ford with the oxen, where they had last seen her on the eighty-fourth and had looked for her on the hundred and fourth. She would come down to it on the hundred and twenty-first, if nothing broke, and that was seventeen.
   248	
   249	Beyond the ford there was the gate that had given her the coat. Beyond that one there was the child.
   250	
   251	She had listened to that chest on the eighty-ninth evening, before she ate, and it had been no worse. They would have looked for her there on the hundred and ninth. That was two days ago already. She would not hear it again until the hundred and twenty-sixth at the soonest.
   252	
   253	Seventeen days, then, in which she did not know. She had told a crew at a fire what late was. She had not told them that the knowing of it never grew lighter, however often a round came out safe.
   254	
   255	She would send word up the road by the first carter going that way: how late she was, and why, and where to send for her if the breathing changed. It was all that could be done from here, and she had done it a dozen times in thirty rounds, and it had never once felt like enough. She had learned, a long while ago, to tell the things that could be done from the things that could only be carried, and to put the second sort where they did not get into her hands.
   256	
   257	She went over the top without turning to see Thornwater below her. There was a mule in that yard that would be standing under three boxes by now. She had read the beast before she left her, as she read anything of hers: a sound heart, and a broad slow bed, and no notion whatever that she had been given away.
   258	
   259	She had told the big one that a person should not name a beast.
   260	
   261	She had never named that one. At the wicket she had learned that this made not the least difference.
   262	
   263	---
   264	
   265	She slept that night in a carters' halting-ring on the far side of the hill, alone, and she was colder than she had been on any road in years.
   266	
   267	It was the mule again. She had lain against that warm brown flank on a good many frozen nights and had never once thought of it as a thing that she owned. She lit no fire. It was a road; and besides, she had her back against a chock-stone and a great deal to go over, and she went over it better in the dark.
   268	
   269	She began with her hands, as she always did.
   270	
   271	They were as they had been. She had mended eleven people at that gate since a morning on a plank, and every one of them had come up under her palms with banks and a bed. She had laid them on a girl's arm a fortnight old and felt eight places take. Nothing in them was missing. She had told the boy so, since he had a right to know what he had not broken.
   272	
   273	There was a piece of her craft in him, all the same. She knew that as she knew a mend of her own on somebody else's leg. She had felt it go along beside her and then go on alone.
   274	
   275	She put no question to herself about what he was. She had heard a small pale person say, by a lantern, that there was a thing she would not write down; she had seen a calfskin with rings on it, and heard a quiet man say *yet*. Those were other people's trades. She had gone into that loft with one thing that she knew, and she had said it, and come down a ladder.
   276	
   277	She thought about the line instead, since that was hers.
   278	
   279	She had crossed it eight times in the grey, where nobody was to see, and one person had seen and held his tongue for twelve days. Each time it had been the same. On one side of a grey stone she was a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, with a reserve like a purse. On the other she was a woman in a dead man's coat who could tie a knot.
   280	
   281	She had not minded it as much as she had expected, and that was the thing that she had gone in eight times to be sure of. A carter with a split palm did not care which side of a stone her hands had last worked on. And on the evening after that noon she had bound a girl's arm inside the line with a man's folded cuff and strips of her own shirt, having nothing else, and the binding had held until six people could get the arm out to where a Tide was a Tide.
   282	
   283	She had told a loft that she was no use on the far side of that stone, and it was true. But she had also learned at that stone how long an arm keeps, bound, and how far a person can be carried. It came to about two hundred yards.
   284	
   285	So she would be at the edge. There would be another of those places; the small one had as good as promised it. Somebody ought to stand on the right side of the stone with her sleeves pushed up.
   286	
   287	She came to the last of it then, which she had been keeping.
   288	
   289	He had tried to pay her at the well. She had seen it coming across the yard, in the way that he carried the book; he was a boy who could not bear to owe, and he had wanted to be rid of the debt before she went, as a person wants a splinter out, without much caring how it is drawn.
   290	
   291	It had been her teacher's saying, about a single morning. She had heard it over a basin, forty times, with a ruler in the woman's other hand. A reading is a thing that the reader does, on a day, in a temper. The first tells you about the temper. It is the third that begins to tell you about the patient.
   292	
   293	He would read himself twice more. He was frightened of what he had found, and he would do it regardless, since he had been told to. Then he would sit on some bench of hers and wait his turn, and say it; and she would be the first living person to hear what the thing that she read with had made of the one body that it could not read.
   294	
   295	She wanted that. She looked squarely at the wanting, with her back against a cold stone, as she had once looked at it on a road out of Oxhollow. She had not forgiven him. She did not expect that she would, quite. But she had once been hungry for sixteen days to know how it was that he could not be read, and she was hungrier now to hear what he had found.
   296	
   297	The road bent in the morning.
   298	
   299	It had gone north from the wicket, and over the low place, and north again. At the first water it turned along the bank, as a carter's road will, and by the middle of the day she had the sun on her left cheek, and by the afternoon it was in her eyes. The water grew. Another came into it, and a third. There were alders, and then a hedge, and a place where wheels had been in and out of the stream for a great many years.
   300	
   301	A little before dusk she heard it working.
   302	
   303	It was a slow heavy knocking, a long way down the valley, with water falling under it. She knew that sound. There would be a wheel at the end of it, and a fence, and a keeper who counted people off a ridge. Beyond the fence there would be a bench, with whoever had sat down on it sixteen mornings ago when her mule should have come in sight.
   304	
   305	She shortened nothing and lengthened nothing. She shifted the rope on her shoulder, and went down toward the sound at the one pace that she had, on the low side of nothing at all.

