editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7142-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:107:There was grass. That was nearly the whole of it. It began at the height of his ankle, on the turf by the spring, and by the middle of the afternoon it stood to his knee, and before dusk to his hip: pale stuff, the colour of dry straw, with a seed-head that hissed against his coat. No stock had cropped it. There was not a fence in sight, or a smoke, or a tree, only long slow swells of ground going away to a sky that seemed to begin at his boots. The wind came over it from the north with nothing in its way, and laid the whole of it over in one direction, like the nap of a cloth under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7143-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:93:"I shall not be a reason. You shut that thing for my sake, and you have kept it shut at a cost that I watched you pay in a river. But I have had four nights in which to consider who was looking in a lane, and I find that I should sooner have her told what may be done to her than be used to keep her ignorant. Strike the second. It is not available to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7144-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:101:"That there's nothing I could put in front of her which she could weigh. I'd be telling her a thing with no name that she has ever heard: that I take pieces of people when they come at me hard enough and mean it, and that there may be room in me for a tenth. What does a person say to that, at night, through a door?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7145-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7146-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:She took up the cheek-strap, and let it fall again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7147-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:25:It was a narrow green place, a good deal greener than anything on the ridge. The water came down it fast and brown over a weir of stakes and stone, and above the weir a channel had been cut away along the bank to a tall grey building with a wheel on its side as high as a barn door. The wheel went round steadily in a white fall of water. There was a fence of split posts about the mill and a dozen roofs, and below the fence a ford, with a flat stone on each bank for the wheels to aim at. Smoke rose straight up from four chimneys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7148-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:105:"I've read one thing with what I had from you, and it was myself. I found no bank in it anywhere, as you found none. I don't know what a bank is like under the hand. I've only your word that there is such a thing." He kept his own hands at his sides. "When you take the boy's wrist, may I set my hand on it too? I want to learn what reaches you through yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7149-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:131:"It is an observation. You may put my initial against it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log:7150:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:167:"I know where you had it. I said it to a loft, with my hand on a ladder." She rubbed her palms together slowly. "It's what I say at every gate when I leave it. I don't ask them whether I may go. I tell them where I'll be, and then it's theirs to carry, and they can't say afterward that they weren't told. I never had it said to me before. I don't care for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7151-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:73:"It's that. The door's shut, and I'm on the wrong side of it from where I keep the thing." He let his arms fall. "It's queer. I've not used it since the ford, and I'd have told you I'd got used to going without. But I always knew where it was. I could feel it in the next room, as you can feel a fire through a wall. There's no next room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7152-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:11:"May I read you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7153-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:105:She had not spoken to him since the yard. She had sat at a board with him, and had eaten a goat; it was not the same thing, and he did not make the mistake of taking it for one. A person may sit at a table with somebody whom she has not let off, as she may mend a man whom she does not like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7154-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:233:"I have a name for it. I want it said to four people before it goes into the box, as he says a line under his eave. Then any of you may call it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7155-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:27:It went from under her on the fourth day, on a sheet of ice where a spring had crossed the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7156-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:93:To the south there were two shapes still. One of them, the one that had stood all day with its arms folded, raised a single arm above its hood and let it fall. Then both stooped, and straightened, and went away over the rim to the south, with nothing in their hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7157-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:119:"I have thought about that too," said Seln. "I could walk up those steps now, with this held over my head. They would take it. I do not think that the fence would come down afterward. A paper that sends six people to a hole in the ground is not concerned with what becomes of anybody who could say how the thing was collected." He paused. "I may be wrong. I have been inside that service, and I do not think that I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-7158-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:181:"After, he is to do everything that he has forbidden himself all day, as loudly as he can, on the far side of that stone. Their whole plan is that nobody on this ground has a Path. The instant that one of us stands outside the fence with something in his hands, and the fence is down behind him, there is no plan. Five more of us may walk out and be what we are." Seln's head turned toward Cael. "They were sent for a case. They were not sent to fight in the dark for it. I know what my own orders would have been, and so do they."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8511-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:193:It was made out of a verb, like the other; it named a thing that went on happening, to whatever came over the line, for as long as it stayed there. It did not say how, and it did not say why. It took *the* in front, which made it one thing, and it would take a place after it without complaint: the Quieting at the Fallow Ring. She could write *inside* before it, and *the edge of*, and *a site of*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8512-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:251:"The Quieting." She made a face. "It sounds like what you do to a baby."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8513-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8514-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:263:"I gave it one a long while back, on an old leaf, when it was only something that another person's hands had caught the edge of. The still place. I never had anything to lay the words on till last night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8515-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:275:"A still place that everything goes around. I've never felt that in anyone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8516-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8517-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:85:"Weather. It's cold on that top a month before it's cold here." Pike dipped his pen. "And shale-backs. The salt crews see them from the cold road, across the valley, on the high ledges. Bigger than a cart, and they eat moss."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8518-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:107:He knew what it would be. A word had been dropped at that plank a quarter of an hour ago, in a voice that was not quite Pike's ordinary one, and Seln collected such things as other men collected debts. He would be asking what a shale-back was, and who had been under one, and what had become of them, and he would get it in fewer sentences than anybody else could have.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log:8519:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:123:"Big. Under. Comes up. Nobody's seen it whole." She rubbed her palms together. "He'd a name for it, the wold-wyrm; I'd not know a wold from a wash. He said that he'd not have gone down to those reeds for a year's salt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8520-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:141:"The edge of the Quieting," she said. "A third time, and no more warning in it than in the others."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8521-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:19:"I asked a question at the plank, two mornings ago, and I have carried the answer for a day and a half, since it concerned nothing that we had yet seen. I asked what a shale-back was, and whether a crew had ever come to harm by one." He inclined his head toward the horn. "He gave me the carters' rule first, and he gave it twice, and I shall give it as he did. 'Never be below them. Never be *loud* below them.'"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log-8522-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:237:*Ninety-sixth day. The Fallow Ring, at the eastern lip of the floor. Sunset.*
