editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34264-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:She took up the cheek-strap, and let it fall again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34265-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:23:"I put that to him also. He said that the salt crews go under them twice a year on the cold road, and walk, and are let alone. The animals are as mild as cattle. A man may pass beneath a herd all day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34266-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:33:Cael looked at Karis. She had the notebook out at last; it had come out of her coat the moment the bar dropped, as though it had been on a spring. She wrote one word at the head of a clean leaf, and looked at it, and drew a single line beneath it. Then she looked up at the woman with the lantern, and he saw the second question arrive in her face, whole, with its grammar. It was the question of what a thing like that could be waiting for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34267-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:111:"It stops short," she said. She was sitting on the top box with the leaf on her knee. "Short of what? I can think of four answers, and I've no way of choosing among them. It may stop short of the fence, or of the people behind the fence. It may be that something lies under the ground at that distance and nowhere else. Or a hundred and ten is simply as near as it ever comes to anything, and the stone is an accident."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34268-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:133:He knew what he was looking at, and it was not anything that he knew about animals, of which he knew very little. It was his own trade. A man set to follow another is given his distance. He is told to keep it, and neither close nor fall back; and it is chosen so that the ordinary noise of the person in front will cover him, and so that one particular thing, if it should happen, will reach him across the gap. He does not act until it does, and he may walk behind a subject for a year and never be given leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34269-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:25:It was a narrow green place, a good deal greener than anything on the ridge. The water came down it fast and brown over a weir of stakes and stone, and above the weir a channel had been cut away along the bank to a tall grey building with a wheel on its side as high as a barn door. The wheel went round steadily in a white fall of water. There was a fence of split posts about the mill and a dozen roofs, and below the fence a ford, with a flat stone on each bank for the wheels to aim at. Smoke rose straight up from four chimneys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34270-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:105:"I've read one thing with what I had from you, and it was myself. I found no bank in it anywhere, as you found none. I don't know what a bank is like under the hand. I've only your word that there is such a thing." He kept his own hands at his sides. "When you take the boy's wrist, may I set my hand on it too? I want to learn what reaches you through yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34271-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:131:"It is an observation. You may put my initial against it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log:34272:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:167:"I know where you had it. I said it to a loft, with my hand on a ladder." She rubbed her palms together slowly. "It's what I say at every gate when I leave it. I don't ask them whether I may go. I tell them where I'll be, and then it's theirs to carry, and they can't say afterward that they weren't told. I never had it said to me before. I don't care for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34273-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:277:"Don't pass," said Lira. She had sat on a landing wall for a week learning carters, and she said it as a rule. "You don't go by a cart on a road. You fall in behind, and you wait until somebody speaks to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34274-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:7:"There's the store behind the chimney," he said. "It's warm; it has the back of that fire for a wall. I keep meal in it." He looked at the three boxes, which Brom and Seln had brought in out of the snow. "Six, for the cold term. And you may lie at this end of the hearth for the standing-money, the five of you, as long as there's paper in my store."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34275-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:131:It was a high bare way between two hills, with nothing on it but frost and a few sheep that belonged to nobody, and she slept each night with her back against the jenny's belly and her feet in her pannier. On the fourth afternoon the ground began to fall. There was a valley under her with a pale road in it, going east and west, and she knew that road; she had gone round a ring of stones by it, thirty times, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34276-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:171:"And the sum. It is written in words, and then again, in a bracket. A person is taught to do that who has been warned that a figure may be altered and a word may not. I was so taught, at a ruled desk, when I was not a great deal older than he is." The mild eyes came up. "The place is given as a description. A day, and a rise, and a road that bends. Every carter in that meadow has a name for it, and our friend at the plank used the name at once. Whoever wrote this either did not know it or was not permitted to put it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34277-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:119:"I have thought about that too," said Seln. "I could walk up those steps now, with this held over my head. They would take it. I do not think that the fence would come down afterward. A paper that sends six people to a hole in the ground is not concerned with what becomes of anybody who could say how the thing was collected." He paused. "I may be wrong. I have been inside that service, and I do not think that I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34278-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:181:"After, he is to do everything that he has forbidden himself all day, as loudly as he can, on the far side of that stone. Their whole plan is that nobody on this ground has a Path. The instant that one of us stands outside the fence with something in his hands, and the fence is down behind him, there is no plan. Five more of us may walk out and be what we are." Seln's head turned toward Cael. "They were sent for a case. They were not sent to fight in the dark for it. I know what my own orders would have been, and so do they."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34279-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:235:He took the southern of its two ruts, since that was the side that the newer bar stood on. It was a trough a foot deep and as wide as a wheel, with turf grown down into it, and it ran straight up the fall of the ground to a stone that he could not yet see. He had a hundred and ninety yards of it to walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log-34280-editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:141:It was there. His boot came down on the frozen earth of the yard as it had come down all morning. But he felt it come down half a beat after it had done so, as a man hears an axe from across a valley after he has watched it fall.
