editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-35.md-137-She was a long time at that one. The mule turned its head and nosed her elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-35.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-35.md-139-The eighth was the longest, and she did nothing in it at all. She let go of the mule and walked three strides into quiet ground and stood there, square, with her arms hanging and her face toward the pale thing on the hill; and he counted a hundred before she moved. He thought that he understood it. She had told four holds' worth of carters, for thirty rounds, that she had no business in that place. She was finding out what she was when she stood in it: a woman in a brown coat, with a mule.
