editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-35.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-35.md-145-He had the book open. He had the day's number written at the head of a clean leaf, and nothing under it. He had entered every crossing of that line since the ninety-fifth evening: five people abreast, and Karis's nine in the dark, and each of the trials in its row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-35.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-35.md-147-He looked at the pencil. It was a finding. She had made it eight times over, by a method, and it was about the line; Karis would have given a week's paper for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-35.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-35.md-149-It was also the only thing that he had ever watched that woman do for herself. She had not done it for a table. She had got up before the light to do it where nobody would be looking, and she had found that somebody was, and had said nothing, and gone to see to a mule.
They went at Lira's pace. She walked it flat, in the great boot, with her right hand on Seln's sleeve and her left tied across her breast; and Cael came behind on a leg that gave him eleven strides and then shut like a fist, as it had done once before, on a green rock in a river. He stood until it let go. It was a count that he knew.

The healer had come too. She had not been asked. She looked at the square brown bar in the grass, and at the stamp on its neck, for about as long as she had looked at five people under a rock; and then she let herself down beside it, by stages, and sat with her knees up and the iron against the small of her back.

"That's cold," she said. She did not move.

"Three in two days," Oryn said, after a while, to the country.

He waited.

"The arm. That was last night, and it was one, and it was the size of two. The throat. The hand. Three mendings in two days." She turned her hands over on her knees and looked at them, and let them lie. "That's a route. Near enough, that's what I carry between a hold and the next one, for the people who feed me and shoe the mule. Three serious hurts and a road's worth of small ones. And I've spent it on a road two days from the nearest roof, on a crew that's not on my circuit, sitting against a piece of iron somebody drove where they'd measured to."

Cael sat across from her, with his leg out, and listened.

"The top gate of my round's eleven days north of here," said Oryn. "That's by the road, at her pace." She tipped her head toward the camp, and the mule. "I'm not telling you for thanks. I've watched you. You count everything that's put in front of you, and that's a figure nobody would have put there."

He had been counting, since a far lip, what the day had cost, and he had supposed the count complete. He had not had this. A woman had added a stop to the only thing she owned, for two reasons of her own, and seven gates that had never been asked were paying for it.

"I'd not have had it," he said. "I have it now."

"Good. Don't do anything with it. Just have it."
She had gone straight to it, on her whole sole, and stood with her nose a hand from the square black writing. Cael came up beside her. The five names were where she had said, on the eleventh line; and over them, where there had been two crews under theirs, there was one that had been written back above.

"It's the wooden foot," said Lira. "The four who never lost a mule. They've gone over us again." She turned on the hearth bench. "We went into a place that you've never stepped over the edge of, and six of us came out. You've just said so twice. And it's cost us a line."

"It has."

Pike was looking up at her from under his own handwriting, and he did not seem displeased to be asked.

"You've read my lines. Every one says what came back, and what was paid on it. That one says the things weren't there and the box stayed shut. A hold comes down to my plank with a job to give, and asks me who fetches what he's sent for. I've a crew that was sent for a thing and came home without it." He held up his one hand. "It's nothing against you. The cloth can't say whose fault a thing was. It can only say what was on the cart."

"So we're worth less for it."

"On the cloth. I never said the cloth was all of it." He looked at the fire for a breath, and then at his book, and he went on to the book, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "I've had that sheet thirty years. Forty-odd crews have been on it, one time and another, and gone off it. Some went home. Some the road had. There's not one of the forty that ever went over a line and came back to tell me so; there's none that I ever heard of who tried. I've a line in here now that says it was done." His finger lay on the leaf. "Six in and six out, and the sixth a healer. I'd a good look for the column to put that in. There isn't one."

"Then make one," said Lira.

"I'll make one the day I know what goes at the head of it."

He said it mildly, and he took up his cup. Cael stood before a sheet of sized sailcloth with eleven lines on it, and found that he was not thinking of the line that they had lost.

He had been a number in other people's books for as long as he could remember. A magister had given him one every week, to the decimal. Vell had kept one for him in a yard. Every one of those figures had been somebody's best account of how much of a thing he was; and here was a book-keeper with one arm, who had never owned an instrument, saying aloud in a room that his own book had a true line in it and was too small to hold what the line meant. Cael did not know what to do with it.

