editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:13:Cael brought it down with his right hand. The left would not close properly on the ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:25:"It can. The arm can't." Her mouth was hard. "So keep that bow up, and somebody look the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:27:Brom had the bow between his knees already. He set his boot in the stirrup a third time, with four fingers that he had not yet let anybody see, and drew, and Cael heard his breath go in through his teeth. He laid a bolt on it, which left three in the sleeve. Then he walked ten yards to the north and stood there with his back to the lantern, a broad dark shape with a box still on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:31:There was one thing more to do before she went under. It was Karis's to do, and Karis could not; she sat in the grass with her two hands at her throat and her eyes on him. She held, as a one-armed man at a plank held, that a thing not said was not a finding; and so he said it for her, aloud, as Pike read a line before he wrote it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:67:He could not read what was in them. He had been able to read that face across a yard since a lane in Ardenmere, and tonight he could not. It was not anger, which he knew, and it was not fear either. She looked at him steadily, as she might have looked at a card that had come up with a name on it which she had not expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:73:He listened for it the whole hour, and he learned what that hillside sounded like at night. The pale grass never stopped. The wind went over it from the north in long breaths, and it hissed and lay down and stood again, so that he was always hearing something come and finding that it was only the country. Twice Brom shifted his feet. Once the mule blew, and once, far off toward the north, a bird got up and cried and settled. There was a small dry ticking, close by, which he could not place until he saw that it was the frost, taking hold on the wet cloth in the heap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:79:It was closed. That was all that he let himself see: skin, where there had been none, and on it two short rows of small pink marks, one row on the upper side and one beneath, each mark no bigger than a barleycorn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:141:He read them, and nodded. She took the leaf back and held the pencil over it, and he watched the point come down to the paper and lift, and come down. He knew what was in that small head. She had been ten strides in front of him with a hound's jaw across her fists, and she had seen a thing arrive that had not come; and after it a crack, and a light, and something that rang. She had nine questions at the least, and she had been waiting all her life for a source like this one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:155:Seln was standing at the lantern's edge. There was grass-seed in his hair and the strap of a locked case across his chest, and his breath had long since come back. He had gone the better part of five hundred yards by himself through that grass with eight hounds on a floor behind him, and come up a lane with a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:163:Brom had come in. He did not wait to be asked. He knelt down heavily beside the sleeping healer, box and all, and laid the bow in the grass with the bolt still on it, and held out one hand into the light, palm up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:175:He washed it himself, from the skin, with his teeth set, and wound a strip of clean linen out of the healer's pannier round it; and he took up the bow again with that bundle and went back to the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:185:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, which was what an hour had left in it. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:191:*Nine hounds, inside. Two dead, both Brom's, with a healer's bow that he had never shot. Seven out, north and south along the line's outside. Not seen after. No instruments were there. No price.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:199:*The read: far lip to the rut. One head turned for it. I had my eyes in the ground when Lira went up on the rim.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:203:*Wind: a burst, north off the lane. The lock.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:213:*Storm: three lanes, in the rut. The read goes to the air for those. That's the calf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:215:*Wind: the second burst. The lock had a hound in it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:235:He read it over. It was the kind of sentence that Karis struck out of other people's notes, since it had a likeness in it. He let it stand, since he had nothing truer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:241:He had thought her asleep. He had put the lantern out to save Karis's three fingers, and the stars had come down close over the grass; she lay on her back under two blankets, with her mended arm tied across her, and her face was a pale shape turned up to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:They went down in the order that Cael had given. Seln was in front with the narrow knife, and Karis behind him with her hand on her coat. Then came the healer and Lira, as close as two people in one sack: Oryn on the right with her shoulder in Lira's armpit, and Lira's bound arm held up against her own collarbone, and the great boot coming down whole at every step like a duck's foot. Brom walked after them with the bow across his chest. Cael came last, four strides behind Brom's box, with Lira's knife in his hand and the read under his soles. He had the only eyes in the file that were not wanted for carrying somebody, and a rear guard needs room to see what it is guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:27:Nine had come home across that floor in no order, and one of them had sat down to scratch. Two had walked onto Brom's pole from the same place, a minute apart, with the second watching the first. Eight had stood by while a bow was loosed among them. They could have cut this lane ahead of the file at any stride of it, and they had not troubled to; they kept their station on the slowest thing that was walking, and waited for it to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:37:He saw then what the room was that he had wanted on the floor, with his hands on a skull. It was a lane, and five people on it walking away from him. He had been carrying the answer since noon; he had stood at a boulder with a foot of Wind going out of him, and thought only of what he had found, and not at all of what it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:97:Seln was five strides beyond her, turning, with the knife coming up; and between Seln and Karis one of the three that Cael had never told apart had stepped out into the lane and stood there, broadside, with its head level. Lira and the healer were five strides on the near side; Oryn had stopped dead, with her whole weight under Lira, and both of them were looking at the thing on the ground. Brom was a stride behind the two women, with the bow at his shoulder already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:107:He could let Wind have him. It would crack like a whip over the lane and set him down beside her with his feet locked for a count, and it would bring eight animals to the ground where Karis was lying, with him standing in the middle of them unable to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:193:They came after him, and they did not come as they had come all evening. There was nothing idle in them now. They came through the grass as seven had come across a river to a seed of fire on a rock, flat out, with the leaders drawing together and the rest falling in behind; and he had the sound of them on both hands, a dry rushing, like a wind that had found its direction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:207:A Storm lane wanted the air, and to have the air he must give up the ground; at that weight the read would carry one or the other, never both. He let the turf go. It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half of the world that he had walked in since the far lip went out from under his soles. He was a boy running up a ditch in the dark, with a flame in front of his face and nothing to tell him where his feet would land.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:219:It was in the second that his left boot came down on a stone. It was a round thing the size of a loaf, bedded in the rut's floor; he would have had it a stride and a half off with the read, and he had not got the read. The foot turned on it, and the calf took the whole of his weight slantwise, along a seam that a hound had made at a ford and a plate of shale had opened on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:243:It was as near as he came that night to being caught. He turned in the rut, with the flame swinging, and they were there: grey, and close, coming up out of the chaff with their eyes shut to slits and their muzzles white with seed. The channel was full of them. He could not go back down it, and he could not stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:245:He gave Wind his feet a second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:269:He had no read. He had a flame at the end of his arm, which showed him ten feet of grass and blinded him to everything beyond. He could not see the stone. He knew where it was as he knew where his own hand was, and he ran down the dark toward the place where a lane ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:281:It was Anchor. It had gone the whole length of that slope with its face to the west and found nothing that it could be sure of. Now it found nine things. They stood in a row directly across his road, a little more than a stride apart, to the right hand and the left, low on the ground, just beyond the stone. Not one of them gave any light. They were places that somebody had fixed and told to stay, as a bar of iron had been told to stay on that same line, three cords to the north. He knew the hand that had set them as he would have known its writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:283:She had laid them along the line's inside edge. That was what her feet had been finding since first light: the last hand's breadth of ground where a point would hold, so close to the place where an Arbiter stopped that an animal coming out would meet it with its next stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:287:There was grass under him that was only grass, and a mule's shape, and a big man down on one knee a yard in front of him with a bow-stock at his cheek. Brom's eye was not on him. It was on something past his hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:291:Cael turned as he fell. The rangy one was in the air over the stone, at the top of its leap, with its forelegs stretched and the flame's light on its teeth, and it was six feet from the nose of the bow. He did not see the bolt. He saw the animal fold in the middle of its spring, and come down on the western side of the stone like a thing dropped off a cart, and lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:5:The sun had gone under the hills while Cael's book was shutting. He had seen the pale thing hold the light on the evening before, from three hundred yards, and had taken it for water; now he stood below its eastern lip, with the rolled edge at the height of his thigh, and saw what it did close to. The long shadows of the stones were gone, and the whole round of it lay level with his hip and gave back the sky, as milk in a pan gives back a window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:29:They had gone out by the east at first light, as Seln had read off bare earth, and they had come home by the west, up the one track on the whole of that hillside which six people had made since noon. He thought of a healer at the far end of that track, counting to ten on a wrist, twice, with her Path running; he did not know that it bore, and he left it for whoever kept findings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:33:It was Seln, and he said it as he would have read out a line from a bill of lading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:45:"There's a bow on that mule," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:63:*One*, thought Cael. He priced it while he counted, since it was done. It took one of two knives off the hill, and the only one of the six who had nothing wrong with him, and against that it put a bow at the head of the lane. There was a third figure in the sum, which he looked at and did not say: something in his own feet could have had that bow on the floor in forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:65:"Round by the south," he said. "On the turf, close in under the lip, and nobody runs. Lira's first, with the knife. Then Karis, and Oryn. I'm behind you. Brom's last, and he's on the floor side." He looked at the hundredweight of paper on Brom's back. "Leave the box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:75:Cael opened the read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:97:It stood on the rolled edge a yard from Brom's right hip, with its head no higher than his belt, and it looked him over as a man looks over a cart that he may buy. Cael had it inside the read from the instant its forefeet touched the lip, and he felt the weight of it come forward, slowly, off the haunches and onto the forelegs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:109:"It can't get its legs under it. Keep close to the edge. If you give it a yard of ground it'll have its spring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:She ran along the rim itself. It was a thing no wider than a roof-ridge, round on top, with the white a finger below it on one side and the turf a hand below on the other, and she went along it as he had watched her go along a ledge under a mountain, close and quick, with her arms a little out. The great boot came down every second stride with a slap. There was no burst in it and no Path, since there could be none; it was a girl of thirteen on the lanes at Fenmark, who had only legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:175:Wind stood up in his own feet to go with her. He put it down, and it was like putting down his own heart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:It was the size of a calf. He bore the long head down until the jaw lay on the floor, with Lira's forearm lying on the floor between the teeth, and he put the whole of his weight on it and kept it there. He had the thing inside the read, every pound of it; and each time that it gathered itself to wrench he had the gathering a breath before the wrench came, in the neck and the shoulders, and leaned his own weight to the place where it meant to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:229:Everything that he carried had come up, as he had not felt it come since a mountain. Compression showed him the skull under his palms and how little it would take. Wind wanted his feet, and hers. And Ember had the place already, as it had had it once in a ford: he was looking into a yellow eye from a foot away, and it would set a point on anything that he looked at for long. *There.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:247:He was thirty inside his time. He came over the lip with a long thing under one arm, out of its cover, and no breath to speak with. He gave the bow to Brom, and stood holding his own knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:261:It was the lip a third time. A hand's height had spoiled the spring of two hounds, and now it gave a man who had never shot a bow something to rest one on. Brom went back over it and knelt on the turf, box and all, and set the stock on the rolled edge and his cheek against the stock. There were four strides and a foot of white between the iron head of that bolt and the eye that Cael was looking into, and one foot more between the eye and Cael's own face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:271:Cael did not move his hands. He had made the thing as still as he knew how to make it, and that was the whole of what he had to give. Nine fragments lay under his refusal, and not one of them could do what was wanted, which was to let a friend try. He looked across thirteen feet at one grey eye over a bow-stock, narrowed, and at a face that would have done anything on earth sooner than loose that string.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:275:There was a slap, and something went by his wrists with a wind on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:291:Brom carried her the four strides, on one arm, with the bow in the other hand, and Oryn was on her knees in the grass before Lira's back had touched it. Both her hands went round the arm, above the place and below it, as they had gone round a shin on a mountain, and her eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:309:The healer had dragged the tail of her own shirt out from under the brown coat, and she was tearing it upward, with one hand and her teeth, into strips three fingers wide. She did not wind them herself. She had lifted Lira's forearm across her own knee, to keep it higher than the heart, and she would not let go of it for anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:313:He wound, and tucked. It was quick, and nobody was gentle, and when it was done the arm was a pale thick bundle that darkened slowly along one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:325:He looked back once. The white lay behind them, a hand higher than the turf that he stood on, and it had not dimmed. One shape was stretched on it by the lip, four strides in, pale on pale, and eight others stood or sat among the feet of the stones. None of them had come a yard nearer while a bow was drawn and a girl was bound; they had watched it as the pale one had watched Brom from the rim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:329:"Seln in front," he said. "Karis behind him. Then you two. Brom, wind that thing again and keep it up. I'm last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:7:"It came. There are ten. I told her all of it on her table before I was off it: the plate, and what I am, and where each of the nine came from, by its Path. She had Brom fetch the leaf, and she read it." He looked at Seln. "I gave the ninth its Path and no more. I told her that I'd used it once on that hill, and that I had looked to see where her eyes were before I did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:45:She had her notebook open on her knee, and she read from it, with a stop for breath at every period.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:49:He understood her, and he saw that the others did. She had spent two years ruling a leaf about what he was, from the outside, with a curve on it and a column, and she had wanted the result of that morning more than she had ever wanted a result; she had written so against herself in the dark. Now there was a thing in him that read how people were built, and she would never ask him to turn it on a wall, or on a stranger, or on herself. She had put that down, in the only voice that she had, before she could so much as begin to wish it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:She gave him the leather sleeve without a word. He counted what was in it, touching each iron head with his thumb: there were five. Then he took down the bow from the peg where it had hung since the ninety-ninth evening and looked along it, as he would have looked along an axle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:61:"There's a hook for this in her near pannier. I'll ask her for it when she's on her feet, and not before; she's had enough taken off her this morning." He hung the bow on his shoulder. "And the carter went down light at first light, with Karis's four lines in his hat for the one-armed man, and the hold's mark under them. So that's gone, and it says six."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:75:Wind was the first, since Lira was asleep six feet from him and it had always leaned her way. It was a dry hiss, like the pale grass of the Fallow going over in a breath of air, and it lived in his feet. The read came after, low and even, under his soles: a millstone turning, heard through a floor. Something ticked in his right hand, as a lamp-wick ticks when the oil is poor, and he knew whose fire that was; and under his breastbone Pressure drew and filled, slowly, like a bellows worked by a tired arm that has a long day's iron still in front of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:117:He lay down, and was asleep inside a minute, as he was anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:175:He walked the count slowly, and on the fourth he gave Wind his feet for the smallest thing that it had, a lean that takes a body a foot off its line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:243:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:247:*Hundred and first day. The yard, at noon, inside the fence. Wind, eleven leans of a foot each, to learn it with the ground late. That is practice, and I am not putting it in the number; the number is what I spend on a road, or in a fight, or on a person. I enter it here so that nobody has to take my word for what's left out.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:295:He slept that night for four hours together, which was four more than the night before. The nine were all there when he woke in the dark, each in its place, with a wall of barley standing over them; and he let them be nine without going along the row. A forge was a forge. The low sound lay where it had lain down at noon, and the boards under his back came to him almost when they should.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:3:Karis read out her second finding on the hundred and sixth morning, and she began it with a table that all of them had seen made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:5:It was the leaf of the trials. She had ruled it fair overnight, in ink, and she held it to the light of the shutter and gave it to them row by row, in the order in which five people had stepped over a grey stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:11:"A Shadow, Bronze, who sought it and found that it was not there to be asked. A Wind: Iron, first rank, who asked for the whole of it and was given a step." Her voice had lost most of its hinge, and what was left of it creaked only on the long words. "And a Tide: Iron, fourth rank, with a count that stopped at four and nothing in her hands. That makes five Paths, and three tiers, and five people who had never before agreed about anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:17:"They read like five," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:19:"They read like five because we are five. Take away the words, and look at what is under them. An Ember can see every place and cannot reach; a Skin knows where the thing is kept and has no room to fetch it from; and you could have done that step again exactly, and there was nothing to take hold of." She touched each row in turn. "In every one of them the doing is all still there, and what answers the doing is gone. It is one loss, told from five chairs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:25:She took up the pen and wrote one word under the five rows. She underlined it, and turned the leaf so that the four of them could read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:35:She said it without any change of voice, as she might have read out the width of a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:59:Cael stood where he was. He had been a row on that leaf since noon on the ninety-sixth, ruled in Karis's hand at the foot of five others, with a numeral against it; and he had filled it for her himself, with a pencil going, in two halves that were each of them true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:61:Karis drew the leaf toward her and studied the sixth row. Then she dipped the pen, and drew one stroke through the numeral that stood at the head of it, and wrote against the struck figure, small and exact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:73:She blotted it, and he watched the ink go dull. He owed her more than that row, and he had known so since a far lip; and he owed it to one person before her, who was pinning a strip of paper to a clay floor with her right hand and had said *later* ten days ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:83:"You," she said to Lira. "Arm. I'll read it before I go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:91:He saw the crease come between her brows. She shifted her right thumb a very little along the outer row, and pressed, and shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:99:"Seven of them are where I'd have looked for them at ten days. They've taken. The eighth is standing." She laid one finger on the lowest mark of the outer row. "There, under that. It hasn't gone bad; it's only slow. That was one mending the size of two, done by a lamp at the end of a day when I'd nothing left to see with, and I priced it as one. I'd say now that I was a little short on that side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:111:"I'll read it at the fortnight," she said, over her shoulder. "That's the hundred and tenth. If it's taken by then, it's taken."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:115:"I did, and it's sixteen: from the ninety-fifth to the morning after your fortnight." The pannier came down. "I can't read your arm from the next gate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:119:"I know who I've got on that road a good deal better than you do." She said it without heat. "And I'll not have spent a night of myself on an arm and then walked off from it in the week that it wanted watching. Those are my five days. You didn't ask for them, and you're not to carry them. Go and shoot your bow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:133:It was a bow, and it was not his kind. His own leaned against the fence, as tall as he was. This was short and thick, an ash stock as long as her arm with a bow of horn and wood lashed across the nose of it, and an iron stirrup, and a long lever underneath. It was very old. Somebody had kept it oiled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:149:"A bow wants one hand," said the bowman, "and a thing that doesn't move."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:189:She sat down on the planks with her back against a post. He did not explain. He stood with his own long bow at the length of his arm and looked down the valley; and by and by she understood that he meant to stand there until she was finished, however long that took.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:205:There were four chips of stone in a row. She had taken them from their numbered twists of paper and set them along the scrubbed board, a hand's breadth apart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:213:She laid a scrap of the thin paper on the first chip and rubbed the side of her pencil across it. A set of fine white lines came up, close and even, all running one way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:245:"I know whose it was. I am not putting hers in the row with mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:253:"It is the same band, cut to the same depth; a fingernail, he said, and I measured it." On the southern strip the lines stood sharp and white. On the northern they were grey and narrow, and in two places the pencil had not found them at all. "That is weather. That is a north wind on the Fallow, taking a little off a stone in every cold term. Now the founding stone." She put its rubbing between the two strips. "It has stood in a north wall for as long as that city has had a gate. Look at how much of the tool is left on it, and then look at how much of that band is left on the north."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:283:Lira came up the ladder at noon and found the bow lying on the planks in its goatskin, and the lean man standing in the worn place himself, with his own left hand in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:311:She shut her mouth on what she had been about to say, since it was true. She had been a Wind since she was fourteen, and she had never in her life sat down without some part of her already on the way to getting up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:323:"Why did you come down?" she said. "With the bow. I never asked you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:339:*What am I becoming, if nobody's measuring.* She had asked that at a fire, and nobody had been able to tell her. She did not know now. But there had never been such a thing before as a Wind who could not run, and might not catch, and shot another person's bow off a rail with one arm; and she thought that she would like to see what that was by the time there was snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:13:They were long strips, each of them as long as a tall man and a hand and a half wide, of the thin paper that she rubbed stones with; and Brom brought them to her one at a time on his two palms. She took the first and read the corner. She had written there, on a floor with the sun going down, the stone's number, and its height, and the way that it faced.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:29:"Hands, and eyes. I shall be short with you; I was short with you in this barn before." She pointed. "He writes, since his is the hand that I can read upside down. Lira holds what I am looking at. You fetch, and you lift. Seln reads me anything that is paper and not stone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:93:She did want to. She had been waiting all her life for a wall that nobody had read. She had crossed a Line for it, and a mountain, with three boxes; she had knelt on that wall with a pencil while the sun went down, and paid for the last two sheets with a quarter of an hour that a friend had no right to give her. It had never once occurred to her that when the wall came she would not be able to count it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:95:There was a boy asleep over her head who now carried a thing that could read how a person was built. She had entered that she knew it was not hers. She looked at eleven strips of paper in a lantern's light, and thought that an instrument was a fine thing to have, and that paper was what she had been given.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:115:"It isn't too many. It's hide by now." She let the hand go. "You've the hook on your belt. Keep it there. That bow's no good to anybody with its hook in my pannier, and I ought to have known it at noon on that hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:127:She put no question about the lines. She had seen a table with somebody else's work on it, and had given it the one look that a person gives to a trade which is not hers; and Cael, who had been read for most of his life by people who could not leave a strange thing alone, thought that he had never seen it done so cleanly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:171:She had been in. She had stood a stride inside that line nine times in the dark, and what she had found there was no quieter than the rest of the Fallow. The grass hissed. There was wind. On the second evening there had been nine animals, and one of them had had its jaws across her fists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:213:She had made each of those leaves by itself, on a different day, for a different purpose, and she had kept them apart in the box. She had never before set the three in a row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:217:She had known the sentence. That was what had woken her. It had a subject, and it had a verb, and it went from the first of those leaves through the second to the third as a straightedge goes through three marks on a board. It was not long. A child could have read it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:221:It was not a finding. Nothing on the three leaves said it; they only lay in a row, and let her say it for them. If she wrote it, it would stand in her hand, in ink, in a box that she meant to outlive her, and whoever opened that box in a hundred years would take it for a thing that she had known.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:239:She turned her leaf round on the board, and said the two words aloud while Cael read them upside down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:265:"A name that is read out to a meadow is on a boat inside the month," said Seln, from the end of the trestle, where he had stood through all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:273:"It is a good word for a report," said Seln. "I should have been glad of more words of that kind, in fifteen years." He considered the leaf. "There will be another name for this in the west, on the docket of a tin. I never read the docket. I do not know what they call it, and I shall not guess."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:177:"I know only the form," said Seln. "*The office notes the anniversary of the enrollee's entry upon the roll.* It was read aloud by the senior clerk, standing. One was not expected to join in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:191:It was only a number. He had been given a great many, by a great many people, and most of them had been wrong; this one was merely true. A line in some register moved down a row. The last time that he had felt it move, he had been in a room with a brass lock on its door and a house's seal on every paper in it. There was no seal here. It had been said to him across a goat, in a grain hold on no map, by a girl with one arm, who had counted toward it over a mountain so that he should not let it go by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:205:"Read it," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:207:He read it to them as it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:229:"From the one-armed man, same as before," the carter had said. "You're to read it, and if it's wrong you say so when you're down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:235:The other line was a long way west of that hearth, on the far side of the Line, in a registrar's daybook, entered with a withdrawal. It had never been in the satchel. A magister had written it on the last day that Cael stood at that counter, and had asked that he should read it there; and he had carried it off by heart, since that was the only way in which he was let to carry it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:257:Seln passed it, and she read it for herself, once, and gave it on to Karis. Cael had written a healer down as a stop, in pencil, a minute before a one-armed man's line came in at the door to say that the country had already entered her otherwise. A clerk's fair copy of that same count was in a boat by now, going west to whoever had put the question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:285:"It's been off the tree longer than any apple on that shelf. That brown you're so fond of came down a fortnight after it, and it'll be meal before the snow's a week old." He had turned, and was reaching up to a shelf over the hearth, into the dark behind a row of crocks; and what he brought down was green, and long, and wrinkled like an old woman's knuckle. "That's the same tree as your sour one, and it's last year's. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:347:He followed the hiss of Wind down to it, and Wind went round. The millstone went round on the other hand. So did the wick, and the bellows, and the held note, and the thin high one; the low sea parted and went by on both sides and closed beyond. He took each of the ten to that place in turn, one after another, with his heart going hard under his own palm. Not one of them went through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:367:*Hundred and second day, night. The loft. Read myself, as she set it: once, alone, on my back.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:377:*Still open. Still real. Now I can see it. That's worse. The tenth fragment lets me feel my own currents, and there's one in the place session nine logged, and it isn't the tenth, and it isn't any of the nine. It was there before Oryn. It was there before Brom's read caught it. I don't know what it is. I know where it is now. Patience is over; observation begins.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:379:He read that through. It was hers to hear before anybody, on a day that she would name, and until then it would lie in a book with its mouth shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:1:# Chapter 37 — Minute Twenty-One
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:5:A man asleep draws twelve to the minute. He had timed Brom against Hesk's clock once, on a road, for want of anything better to do, and had kept the figure. Twelve to the minute made sixty in five, and two hundred and forty in the twenty that she had named; and he lay on a scrubbed plank with a healer's hand over his breastbone and began at one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:19:He had thought about what it would be. He had lain awake over it in a loft, with the book under his hand. He had been read by a great many things, and the only one of them that he could do himself went down: it opened under his boots like an eye and took the weight of whatever was beneath, a pace and a half, straight into the ground. He had supposed that a healer's reading would do the same, and that he would feel it come down into him from her palm as a plumb-line comes down a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:31:He could feel her keeping time. He had not expected that, of all things: that he would lie under an instrument and be able to tell that it was a person. A brass plate in a district hall had waited over him for eleven seconds, and he had not felt it wait; an assessor's panel had read him across a floor, and he had got nothing from it but a number on a slate. This one limped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:55:*Eighty-four.* Something in his feet turned over like a sleeper who had heard his name. It was Wind. He did not tell it no, and he did not let it rise. He let it turn over, and it settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:61:"Everyone I've ever had my hands in, this is the part where I know." She said it as a finding about herself. "Iron Skin, you feel the banks first — dense, walled, everything held. Ember, the banks are a fire's, hot at the edge. Wind you barely feel the banks at all, the water's too fast. But there's always a bank. It's how you know which river." A pause, long, her current going along Ember and stopping at the ignition point and lying there as if it had found a stone in a bed. "I can't find one here. I keep putting my hand where the bank ought to be and there's more water."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:67:Compression lay through the whole of his frame, and it did nothing at all; it had been built to wait for a blow, and no blow came. The read wished to open. It wished it very much. There were two hands on him and a square woman standing over a table, and it could have told him the weight of her to a pound and which way she leaned. He left it shut. He did not hold it shut. He found, after a while, that there was a difference, and that it lay in whether his jaw was tight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:95:It was in the thirteenth minute that he understood what was strange about her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:105:He turned that over, slowly, at a man asleep's pace. She had watched him go up a hill with fire in his fist, in a place where she could not count to four, and she had asked him whether his calf was worse than an arm. She had read him afterward and found nothing, and said so, and left his leg in its cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:113:She had gone quiet. He counted through the fifteenth minute, and the sixteenth, with nothing over him but her breathing and the slow going-along; and he felt it change. It was no weaker, but it had been a walk, and now it was a search. She went down one side of him and stopped, and came back a hand's breadth, and tried it again from a little farther in, as she had tried a stone with her feet at dawn, eight times. She was looking for an edge: under his heart, and along his arms to the fingers, and in the long muscles of his back. She was not finding one, and she would not leave off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:125:"I can feel that there's movement. More than one. I can't count them — I can't find where one stops. There's no channel. Everyone has a channel; I feel the banks and I know the Path. I can't find the banks in you. I've looked for twenty minutes. It isn't that you're broken. It's that I can't find anything shaped like the thing I read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:131:He was at two hundred and twenty-eight. That left one minute of her twenty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:147:And along the slow current that she kept, the one that set out from her right hand and came home to her left, he could feel a shape. It was not his. It was the way she went: the keeping of time, and the feeling ahead for a bank, and the patience of it, which would walk the length of a person eight times sooner than guess. It was the thing that she read with. She had laid the whole of it open on him for twenty minutes, in earnest, at a cost, and she was pressing it against him now harder than she had pressed it yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:163:So he knew which was right, and he chose against it. He was exact about why. It was not that a leaf permitted him; a thing written down beforehand is only a thing done on purpose. It was that he wanted to know what he was more than he wanted to be in the right, and the one instrument that might tell him was standing at his breastbone. He would answer for it within the minute, on this table, to her face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:186:He read it through, from the top, with her hand still on him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:198:There was a third. He knew what it was. It was already written, on a leaf, in a loft across the yard, in his own hand, with last night's date at the head of it; and he did not set it in the row with the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:218:She did not take her hands away. He felt the right one move on his breastbone, by the width of a finger, toward the place where a cup had filled. It stopped there. She could not read him. She had said so five times. But she had been walking the length of something for twenty minutes, and she knew its gait by now as he knew hers; and it was not going as it had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:226:He could have let her think so. He saw it open in front of him, as easy as a gate: it would have been a healer's hands doing something that they had never done before, to a boy that they could not read, and she would have carried it north with her and turned it over at every hold for a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:11:Karis had the leaf ready, and she read it standing, as a one-armed man read a line under an eave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:15:She lowered the leaf, the shortest thing that she had read out in six days; she had plainly cut it down from something a good deal longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:61:"I have four things in the box," she said at last. "A name. Five rows that are one row. A floor that is older than my oldest stone. A bar of iron. I should swear to any of them in front of a seat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:97:He had had the same three in a row since a far lip, and had looked at them on a good many nights, and he had never heard them said. It was a different thing to hear them in that small exact voice, in a cold barn that smelled of hens.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:115:The healer read the arm at first light on the hundred and tenth day, in the yard, with the frost still white on the well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:123:She shifted her thumb to the lowest mark of the outer row, and pressed, and counted a second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:199:"For the next one," she said. "They never have a rope fit to lead with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:221:She shot from the rail first. Cael could see only the nose of the bow over the fence, and one goose feather; he heard her breath go out, and then nothing at all, for so long that Brom shifted his feet and one of the carters began to say something and was hushed. Then there was the slap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:233:There was a scuffling on the planks, and the nose of the bow went down out of sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:237:It was the only one that he gave her. Cael understood it: there was to be a single bolt from the knee, at sixty paces, with a hold looking on, and no second if it went wide. Wind had come up in his feet to go with her, as it had on a rim; and he heard it come, and let it lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:251:On the gate-walk a lean man lifted two fingers from the stave of his own bow, an inch, and laid them back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:253:Brom had the healer's bow on his shoulder. He looked from it to the fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:267:She was alone on the walk, and that was new. The lean man had gone down to his supper while there was still light in the west, which Cael had never known him do; and he had left a girl with one arm sitting against the southern post, and the yard bow lying spanned on the planks beside her knee. She had the whole valley in front of her, grey going to blue, with the frost coming down on a mile of pale grass and a white stone standing in it; and it came to him that in thirty years nobody but one man had sat where she was sitting at that hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:277:He gave it to her in order, and low, and he began by telling her that it had not started on a white floor. It had begun at noon on that day, a cord north of the grey stone, while five people were looking at what they had lost and got back; he had opened the read a pace short of the line, and walked, and it had run across the stride without a break. He had gone twenty strides in, to a boulder, and asked for a foot of Wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:281:"Of yours. Not half an hour after you'd stood at that stone and asked for all of it, and been given a step." He kept his eyes on the valley. "And I walked back and gave Karis a row for her table that had two true halves in it and wasn't the truth. I had three reasons. One was the healer, who knew nothing. One was sealed. The real one was that I'd found a thing that I couldn't finish the sentence for, and I've never been able to hand anybody half a sentence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:301:Lira turned her head at last, though it was too dark to read her face, and he could see only the pale shape of it and the sling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:323:"There's one thing more," he said. "I read myself, on the night of the birthday. I found something. I can't give it to you. It's the healer's to hear before anybody; that was her price, and I'm paying it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:25:"There's a Wind's, and that one is Lira's. There's an Iron Skin's, which is Brom's, and an Ember's, which came from Karis. You know those three better than I do, since you've had your hands in them. Then a Pressure's, from a Bronze at Ardenmere, and a Compression's, from a fighter on a circuit. A Storm's, from a Gold. An Anchor's, from a hand whose face I never saw. A Blade's, which a friend gave me in a bout that he had asked for." He drew one slow breath. "And a Shadow's. That one I keep shut, for the sake of the person it came from, and I'll tell you its Path and nothing more. I used it once on that hill, for ten strides, in the lane. I looked first to see where your eyes were."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:31:"That's what you saw on the hill, then: the crack was the Wind's, and the fire on the knife was the Ember's. They answer in there, all of them, as they answer anywhere. I don't know why, and nobody does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:35:"This morning there's a tenth." He kept his eyes on her face, which hung over him upside down and grey. "It wants a real thing done to me for a long time, at a cost, by somebody who means it, and you had told me at Oxhollow what the long reading was: both hands, and all of you, for minutes. I knew when I lay down what it was likely to be. At the end there was a place in me standing open, and the thing that you read with was at the door. I could have kept that door shut. I'm better at shutting them than at anything else I do. I opened it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:57:"There's a leaf," said Cael. "I wrote it last night in the loft, before I had so much as crossed your yard. Everything that I knew is on it, and what I had made up my mind to do. You ought to read it before you answer, since it's worse than what I've told you and it's truer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:71:"There's a book in that loft with a leaf in it that I'm to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:87:"That it was wrong." Brom's eyes stayed on her and kept away from the plank. "Each of us, one after another, in our own words. He said we were right. He said the right thing was to come across this yard and knock, or to tell you this morning before you'd washed your hands, and that he would do neither, and that he'd tell you the minute it was over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:97:She shut the door and let the peg fall. She did not take the stool. She carried the book to the sill, into the square of light, and laid it open on the strip of canvas among her own things, between the shears and the horn cup; and Cael thought that he had never seen an odder row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:99:She read it as she went along a bench. She went through the whole of the leaf once, at her own pace, with her lips still and no change in her face. Then she went back to two places, and he knew which they were by where her thumb lay in the margin. The first was a third of the way down, where a boy had written that his reasons were good ones, and had then put down the real one. The second was the short paragraph beneath it, about who would pay, and that one she read with her lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:115:"And your four told you. I've sat with that a minute, and I find it's the part I mind most. It wasn't a thing you did in the dark, by yourself, without anybody to say what it was. You'd been told its name by four people, and you'd agreed with them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:125:"I can read. It's on your leaf." She shut Hesk's book on the straw. "Say it to me again when it's cost you something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:135:"I've spent a mending. I knew that I would; a long one costs that, and I'm no emptier than a long one leaves me." She unfolded her arms and looked at her palms. "Past that I can't tell you, since I can't read myself. No Tide can. So I'll learn it as I learn everything. There are five on that bench after noon, and the first of them will tell me whether these are what they were. If they come back short, you'll hear it from me before you hear anything else today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:157:"So I couldn't read you because there was nothing in you shaped like a Path for the reading to find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:161:"I don't know either. I'm a healer, not a theorist." Her palms went together, slowly, as they did when she had had them in cold water. "What I know is you've now got a piece of the thing I read with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:173:"You've been a thing that nobody could read since you were fourteen. I've stood at that door five times and once more for twenty minutes, and I've a list to keep, and I came all the same." For the first time that morning he heard something in her voice that was not arithmetic. "I want to know what you find, and I want to be the one you tell."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:181:"Good." Oryn gave him the book. "Now lie down again. Not for me. You're the colour of the wall and you've been standing on that calf for a quarter of an hour, and I've just been told I'm a patient's source, and I'd like a minute to sit with the second thing before I go and eat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:185:She had her minute. He gave it to her with his eyes on the rafters, and nothing in that room moved but the light on the sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:195:She read the leaf a third time, since there was nobody to see her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:199:She had been lied to on that table more times than she could count. People lied to a healer as they lied to nobody else: about how long it had hurt, and what they had lifted, and whether they had kept the arm still. She knew what to do with a lie. She set it to one side and read the body.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:217:She put the question as she would have put it of a wound: where, and how deep, and what was standing behind it. She had felt nothing go. Her reserve was down by one mending, which was the price of the work and no more. She flexed her fingers on the leather. They were the hands that she had come in with, so far as hands could tell their owner anything, which was not far.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:249:She did a second after it, a woman's thumb that had gone bad round a thorn, and read the other three and told each of them the truth. Nothing came back short. She had her answer before the shadow of the fence reached the trough, and it was the same that he had given her: it did not show.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:269:She went back to her room. She had a bench to pay that was owed five days now, and a round behind it that was owed a good many more; and a boy in that hold had a piece of her craft in him that he did not know how to use. She was hungry to hear what it would find, and she did not take that for forgiveness. She had been hungry to read him for sixteen days, and it had not made her fond of his ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:It was the hardest cold that they had yet walked in. The ruts of the valley road had frozen as the last grain cart left them, with every hoof-mark standing up like a thing cut in iron, and the pale grass on either hand was white to the knee. Their breath went ahead of them. On the gate-walk a lean man stood with his long bow at the length of his arm, as he had stood on every morning that they had spent behind that fence; and when Lira looked up, he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had nothing in her right hand, and she lifted two of her own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:25:The row of white stones stood whole along the lip, as the hold had set it, and the water went over the sill no more than shin-deep, with a rim of ice along both beaches. Seln crossed first, and then Karis, who lifted her coat. Brom led the mule down to the edge on her off side, where he had walked all morning, and she set her forefeet on the wet rock and would not move them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:49:It was the same thing that she had stopped at the well. He wished to have three readings done in three nights, so that he could set them down at her first bench and stand square. She had told him to read it frightened, and to read it when he was not. She had said nothing about reading it in a hurry; that was a third temper, and it was the one that he had been in when he lay down on her table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:51:He took his fingers off his wrist. The listening went on at the edge of his thought, quick and slow, and he let it go on, as a miller lets a mill; he would read himself on a night that was only a night, and until then the debt could stand where she had put it. It was the first that he had ever left standing without picking at it. He wrote one line, and gave the watch to Brom at the turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:59:It was the fourth time that he had looked down on that town from high ground. He had learned on the first that it was a river, and a ferry, and two inns, and a smith, and a meadow with no wall. On the others he had learned to read the meadow as he had once read a board, since it changed with every week, and whatever was true of that country showed there first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:61:The cold had been at it. Where forty fires had burned when the roads were dry, he made out twelve, drawn in close about the great ash, and half the trodden grass had gone back to white. The ferry's cable stood across the water like a chalk line, with frost on it. The scow lay on the near bank. A brown gelding with one white foot was walking his circle in the capstan-shed's doorway with a blanket on his back, and Cael was glad to see that somebody had thought of the blanket. Both inns were smoking. There was a skin of ice along the eastern bank as wide as a cart, and beyond it the river ran dark and fast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:109:It was the eighth line under their heading. Cael had read it on a scrap of bark in a grain hold, across the bones of a goat, and he knew every word; but he had never heard it said. Pike gave the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the broker's posting. He said that it was taken and witnessed. He said what had not been found there, and that the crew had come back. He gave the count of those who went in and of those who came out, which was the same count, and at the end of it he said that no price was paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:153:"You've read my lines. Every one says what came back, and what was paid on it. That one says the things weren't there and the box stayed shut. A hold comes down to my plank with a job to give, and asks me who fetches what he's sent for. I've a crew that was sent for a thing and came home without it." He held up his one hand. "It's nothing against you. The cloth can't say whose fault a thing was. It can only say what was on the cart."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:171:"It is for what has been read and put back," said Karis, very low. "The girl at the next boards told me, on the ninetieth night. A captain takes a slip down, and reads it through, and hands it in again; and if it is a slip that weighs, he hangs it there, so that the next captain knows it has been weighed and by whom. There is hardly ever anything on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:179:"You'll read it whether I give it you or not. You read the last one off my plank before I'd lifted the stone." He kept one finger on its corner. "It came up from the landing the morning after I'd said your line. The clerk brought it at a trot. His master had had it lying by since the same sack as the other, sealed up inside the same wrapper, with a bag counted and a word of direction on the outside: that it wasn't to be posted till the first was answered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:183:"In the broker's strong-box, with his seal on it, and him not knowing what was in it. He says so. I believe him; he's not a man who'd keep three hundred marks shut up for a month if he knew that he had them." Pike took his finger away. "I said it to the meadow, twice. Nobody scratched. Then Teague came down off his fence for flour, and read it, and hung it where you found it. That's eight days it's been on my nail."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:185:Cael picked the paper up. It was the same weight as the other, which lay folded in Hesk's book; it had the same pricked margin, and one word standing alone at its head, with a stop after it. He held it so that the four of them could see, and read it out low, a stop at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:219:"Can it be read at our own fire?" said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:221:"It can be read where you like. It's been said." Pike pushed the paper an inch toward him along the bench. "Bring it back, or bring it marked. I'll be here till the fire's banked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:3:The healer came up the loft ladder that night for the first time, and looked at a wall of barley with a row of blankets under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:11:Cael gave it to them standing. He gave it as he had given it to one person on a gate-walk an hour before, in the same order and in fewer words: the noon of the ninety-sixth, and a read that had run across a stride, and a foot of Wind at a boulder; a row for a table that had been two true halves; and that he did not know why any of it was so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:13:"Lira's had that since dusk. The rest of you have it now. It's fourteen days late, and I'm sorry for every one of them." He looked at the square woman by the ladder. "And there's one thing more that I have, from the night of the birthday, which none of you is getting. I read myself, as she set it. What I found is hers before it's anybody's. I'm telling you that it exists, so that nobody learns later that I sat on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:27:"I have a row to amend," she said. Her voice was whole. "*Stated by him, not tested by me* stands, since it is still true. I am adding beneath it: *tested by him, at noon on the 96th, unwitnessed; told on the 110th*. And now I want something that I have never had. I have had that hillside for fourteen nights in five heads, and not one account of it on paper. I want what each of you saw. In the order in which you saw it. Nothing that you supposed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:33:"A light," said Brom. "Going up that hill the wrong way, with a noise on the end of it like a glass. And every dog on the slope turned its back on us and went after it. I'd a bow up and nothing whatever to shoot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:119:It lay there for as long as it takes to count two. It was the first time that Cael had ever seen her touch that man, who had not been hurt since he crossed the Line and had given her no occasion; and Seln stood quite still under it. Then she took the hand away, without a word, and went down the rungs into the dark. Cael could not have said whether a tired woman had steadied herself at a ladder's head, or whether it had been something else. He did not guess. He had heard her say, not half an hour before, what she did with a thing that she could not read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:141:Vastin received the broker's completion report as a courtesy copy, forwarded through a registry office that had no interest in any board east of the Line, in the second folder of the morning, marked *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered*. He read it twice — a broker's report from a town on no registry map, on his desk with no routing note — and did not know why it had reached him, and filed that, in the register of a man who had never once in forty years pretended to knowledge he lacked, as the whole of what he knew about it. He returned the copy to its folder unannotated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:153:"Write, then. I've read your hand before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:159:She read it upside down, with her lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:169:"You said that you'd say when, and you go in an hour. I read myself on the night of the hundred and second, as you set it, alone and on my back. I found—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:177:"I do. I never said that I wanted the first thing you saw, the first time you looked." She put the flask into the pannier. "How many times have you read yourself?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:189:"And you've not gone back to it in eight days. I'm not asking you why, since I can see why from here. So you'll go back. You'll read it three times, on three different days, with a night between each. Read it frightened, and read it when you're not, and then you'll know what's there and what was only you." She buckled the strap. "After that you tell me, all of it at once and in order. You don't give me a piece of it now because I'm leaving and you'd like to have paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:199:"You've written it, and I've one thing to put under it." She took up the coil of rope and hung it on her shoulder. "You've four people up that ladder who'd say yes to you before you had finished asking. They're yours, and they'd let you, and that's no reason at all. Don't read anyone on the road to find out whether you can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:203:"Asking's the least of it, and it's not the whole. You put your hand on a person when there's something to read *for*. I've never yet laid a finger on anybody to see whether my fingers worked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:He stayed at the well until the grey had come into the east. Then he turned back sixty leaves, to a line that he had set down under an inn's eave on the forty-ninth night, when he had counted himself free. He read that through, and came forward again to the clean leaf, and wrote the one that had to stand across from it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:257:She went over the top without turning to see Thornwater below her. There was a mule in that yard that would be standing under three boxes by now. She had read the beast before she left her, as she read anything of hers: a sound heart, and a broad slow bed, and no notion whatever that she had been given away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:293:He would read himself twice more. He was frightened of what he had found, and he would do it regardless, since he had been told to. Then he would sit on some bench of hers and wait his turn, and say it; and she would be the first living person to hear what the thing that she read with had made of the one body that it could not read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:295:She wanted that. She looked squarely at the wanting, with her back against a cold stone, as she had once looked at it on a road out of Oxhollow. She had not forgiven him. She did not expect that she would, quite. But she had once been hungry for sixteen days to know how it was that he could not be read, and she was hungrier now to hear what he had found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:27:Her page was a single leaf, and she had been two years in the making of it. It had a curve on it, for how quickly each of the nine had come, and a column for what each had cost him afterward; and at its head, in her small exact hand, stood three words with a rule under every one. She had never in his hearing called it more than a model. He had its three words by heart, and so had the others, since she had read them out on the night that the eighth was priced.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:49:They read it. She was already writing again, and the second leaf came out after the first and was laid beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:69:"I've told her nothing. She has put her hands on me four times and found no banks, and she watched me go up a hill with fire in my fist, and in all that time she has never asked me one question that wasn't about a hurt. She thinks that she is going to lay me on a table and read me. If something is taken from her while she does it, she'll not have been told that it might be. She can't say yes to it, and she can't say no."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:175:"She asked to read you," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:187:"I'm going to do it," he said. "I've heard every one of you, and you're right. The right thing is to cross that yard tonight; or, failing that, to tell her in the morning before she has laid a hand on me. I know that. I am going to lie down on that table and say nothing, and tell her the minute that it's over. I want you to know that I chose it with all of this in front of me. Nobody talked me into it, and nobody was kept from talking me out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:219:The flame had gone down to a blue bead, and he read the leaf through once by it, quickly, from the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:221:It was not a good page. He had hoped a little that it would look better written; that somewhere between the pencil and the paper it would turn into the kind of thing that a careful person does. It had not. It read as what it was: a boy wanted something, and had found that he could not have it honestly, and was going to take it and pay afterward; and he had set down the price first, in a hand that would be dry by morning, so that he could never claim to have been surprised.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:223:That was all that the last line meant. A registry had read him for years, and written down what it found, and had never once told him beforehand what it was reaching for or what the reaching might cost him. He was about to reach for somebody else in the same way. The one thing that he had which that office had not was a leaf with tonight's date at the head of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:229:The bead went out. He lay down between two sacks of barley with the book under his hand; and in the dark Wind leaned a little toward where Lira was breathing, and the heaviest of the nine shifted, like a sea a long way off, and was still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:239:It was small. There was one window, in the eastern wall, a square of grey with its shutter folded back, and a bench under the window. A table stood in the middle of the floor, a scrubbed plank on two trestles, long enough for a man to lie on, with a three-legged stool at its head. Along the sill, under the light, a strip of canvas had been unrolled, and on the canvas her things lay in a row: the tape on its peeled stick, a flask, shears, a hank of clean linen, a horn cup, a bone needle. He had seen them come out of a pannier in a dozen places. He had never before seen them set out in her own order, where her left hand would find each of them without her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:249:Nine things lay in him, and he went along them as he went along anything at the start. Wind was in his feet, and still. Pressure sat under the breastbone. The read was shut, as it had been since a rut. Compression lay in his frame, and Ember in his right hand, with nothing to look at. The bright quick one had asked about the table's edge when he lay down, and Anchor about the sill, and he had told each of them no. The heaviest lay low, like weather that has blown itself out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:31:"Tonight. Tomorrow at noon, and tomorrow night. Then the morning after, and we're in by dark." Brom pointed. "That's four. Six to a row."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:37:He had Seln lay the pieces out on the lid of the middle box, four rows, and Cael saw the healer's eyes go along the first of them. She had said at a stake that six was wrong. There were six heaps in that row, of a size, and the sixth had not been set a little apart, as a guest's is.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:41:It was a strange afternoon. They spoke of a good many things. Lira asked how far a mule could smell a dead dog, and the healer told her. Brom wished to know whether a bow was meant to pull to the left or whether that was him. Karis wrote that she would open the box under a roof and not before, since there was wind, and held it up; and they agreed that there was wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:53:*Ninety-seventh day. The stone. Two mended at noon, and nothing came. My calf read a fourth time, and left in its cloth. Five bolts. Bread in sixths, for four meals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:87:She knew what that was. She had read it in his book before she ever saw it, in a boy's cramped writing, with *sealed* beside it and a mild man's name left out. She had seen it since, a handful of times, and never once in front of anybody who did not already know. It had gone by her elbow like a draught, and then he was on the dog.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:103:She had not got that from anything he said. She had it from his face, a foot from her own, over a dog's head. There had been a thing in it that she had not seen before in all the years that she had read him across a floor. He had been *choosing*. A person does not choose against a thing that he does not know he can do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:145:He had the book open. He had the day's number written at the head of a clean leaf, and nothing under it. He had entered every crossing of that line since the ninety-fifth evening: five people abreast, and Karis's nine in the dark, and each of the trials in its row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:167:She had not known how much of walking was done with the hands. Her balance was all on one side. When the wind pushed her she put out the right and nothing answered it from the left; when she stepped over a rut she felt the tied arm start, under the grey wool, and stop against the knot. It did that forty times in the first hour. It was like walking beside a dog that keeps going for the same gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:221:It was the first flame that any of them had lit on the Fallow road since the way out, and Cael noticed that nobody discussed it. They were a day west of a grey stone; the ground under them was only ground, and a flint was not a declaration. Seln struck it. He had gathered dead stems of the pale grass by the armful, since there was nothing else in that country to burn, and they went up with a hiss and wanted feeding every minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:281:The second thing took him the rest of the mile. A woman was walking on the low side of a mule, six feet from him, who had added a stop to the only thing she owned in order to read a boy whom she could not read; and she had not the least idea that the boy might come away from her table with a piece of what she read with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:287:He had no business at that gate. He kept the western one, and in three stays Cael had seen him leave his platform once, to watch six people go out by the east; and he was standing beside the east gate now, with his bow at the length of his arm, like a man who has been coming to the same place at the same hour for five days. He looked at nobody but Lira. He watched her come up the last fifty yards on the flat of her foot, with a strip of grey wool across her and a hand tucked under her collarbone, and he did not lift two fingers from the stave. He took off his cap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:339:She said it as she would have said a weight. He felt it go into him like a key. She had told him that at a ford: when he was not broken she would run the long one, and she wished to feel it not read him properly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:347:"I meant—" He did not know what he had meant. He had lain on a table at Oxhollow and felt an instrument close on air, and he had wanted, with a wanting that he had not written down, for this one to close on something. "Will you get anything?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:23:It had been right. A bow at the lip had been worth more than a second knife, and he would have gone again. He noted only that it came out the same each time, and that somebody else always paid. He had no column for that, and he found, a little to his surprise, that he would have liked one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:61:The big man came down out of the north when the east had begun to grey, walking as a person walks who has stood since midnight, with a bow in his two hands and a rag round one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:77:He did not read the stamp; he knew what it said. He let his eye rest on it as one lets a hand rest on a latch. A man answers for what he takes: he had said that at noon. Iron of that pattern is counted out across a board by a storekeeper who does not look up, and signed for in a book with ruled columns, and carried away in a case with a strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:83:It was a tin, as long as his forearm, with a cap at one end and a lead tag crimped through the hasp. He had it under his arm. He was young, in the memory, with a satchel and one key, and he was carrying the tin from one room to another; that was the whole of the errand. A junior was permitted to break the tag in the second room, before a senior, and to draw out what was in the tin and count it against a docket. He was to count. He was not to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:87:He had not read them. But nobody can count eight sheets, corner by corner, and see nothing. He had seen a ruled stroke down the left of each that he took for the Line. He had seen roads beyond it, drawn in dashes, as they were drawn on the printed sheet that lay in a box at the camp; only these had run on much farther before they stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:153:"You've not read it," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:157:"I've read him three times."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:171:"I can't mend what I can't read. That's the rule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:215:"I shall say first what this is worth," he said. "It is one man's memory of a thing that he was told not to read. I carried a case once, between two rooms. There were eight sheets in it, of the country east of the Line. I counted them. I saw what a person sees who is counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:225:He had drawn it in charcoal. Cael saw a row of short strokes going across the thin paper from left to right, and two days beyond a small square they bent, and went about a neat black ring, and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:273:Cael read them twice. They were the whole of it, and there was not a verb in them that reached past what six people had seen with their eyes. A map had a blank where this place was. A bar of iron had a stamp.

