editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:93:"A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:97:It stood on the rolled edge a yard from Brom's right hip, with its head no higher than his belt, and it looked him over as a man looks over a cart that he may buy. Cael had it inside the read from the instant its forefeet touched the lip, and he felt the weight of it come forward, slowly, off the haunches and onto the forelegs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:109:"It can't get its legs under it. Keep close to the edge. If you give it a yard of ground it'll have its spring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:145:They were at the south. Fifteen yards on along the kerb two grey shapes were standing on the grass, shoulder to shoulder, with their heads level. They had gone ahead on the white and dropped off the rim where there was nobody under it with a pole, and they stood now on turf, where a hound has all the spring that it was born with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:261:It was the lip a third time. A hand's height had spoiled the spring of two hounds, and now it gave a man who had never shot a bow something to rest one on. Brom went back over it and knelt on the turf, box and all, and set the stock on the rolled edge and his cheek against the stock. There were four strides and a foot of white between the iron head of that bolt and the eye that Cael was looking into, and one foot more between the eye and Cael's own face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:315:"That stays above her heart." She took Lira's left wrist and laid it against Lira's own right collarbone, and shut Lira's right hand over it. "You hold that there. You don't let it down to balance, and you don't put it out if you fall. If you fall, you fall on your face."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:105:She had not spoken to him since the yard. She had sat at a board with him, and had eaten a goat; it was not the same thing, and he did not make the mistake of taking it for one. A person may sit at a table with somebody whom she has not let off, as she may mend a man whom she does not like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:233:"I have a name for it. I want it said to four people before it goes into the box, as he says a line under his eave. Then any of you may call it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:15:Lira was in front of him, and he watched how she went. The ankle was taped, and under a healer's order still, so that she set the great boot down whole at every stride, heel and toe together, and rolled nowhere. She had done it for fifteen days on turf. On frozen ruts it was a different trade. Twice in the first mile the boot skated, and twice her right arm went out and her left stayed where a knot held it; she had been told that she was to fall on her face sooner than put that hand out, and he saw that she had taught the hand to believe it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:141:It was there. His boot came down on the frozen earth of the yard as it had come down all morning. But he felt it come down half a beat after it had done so, as a man hears an axe from across a valley after he has watched it fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:243:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:293:He carried eleven sacks more before the light went, on feet that told him of each rung a little after he had climbed it, and he did not fall off the ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:67:*Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:161:"You could. And if she puts a foot in a rut, you come off, and you come off onto that." Oryn had looked at the sling. "A fall off a mule's a fall. You walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:199:*You were told. I shall fall more carefully.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:The boy was on the mule's other side, at the off shoulder. He was breathing to her beat, as he had learned to on a road while she was not looking, and every so often he stood still for the space of a breath with his weight on the right leg. She had counted his strides between. It was eleven when they left the stone. By the spring, where they halted for the night, it was nearer forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:219:They had a fire that night, at the spring, and Seln made the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:267:Cael noticed it as a person notices that a noise has ceased. He had been walking at the mule's shoulder since first light, across the last of the pale grass, and he reached for the number of his strides and found that he had none; he had not stood still since the spring. He tried the calf on purpose, a long step and then a quick one. It was sore, and it was a leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:185:"I have something to show," he said. "I should like it shown where the iron is. It is three cords, and she may take my arm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:213:Seln had laid the calfskin on the turf, with a stone on each corner, where the stake's shadow did not fall across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:97:She shut the door and let the peg fall. She did not take the stool. She carried the book to the sill, into the square of light, and laid it open on the strip of canvas among her own things, between the shears and the horn cup; and Cael thought that he had never seen an odder row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:235:She had eaten, and had her two hours on her back, with the door shut and the book on the sill. She came out and washed to the elbow in the bucket, and she washed more slowly than she ever did, as a carter walks who thinks that his axle may be cracked and would sooner not hear it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:249:"So I cannot tell you how it was made, since I have nothing to compare it to. But a thing may be dated without knowing how it was made, if it has stood in the weather, and that has." She took up the cup of wands. "I wrote down which way every stone faced. I did it from habit, and it is the best thing that I did on that floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:93:"I shall not be a reason. You shut that thing for my sake, and you have kept it shut at a cost that I watched you pay in a river. But I have had four nights in which to consider who was looking in a lane, and I find that I should sooner have her told what may be done to her than be used to keep her ignorant. Strike the second. It is not available to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:101:"That there's nothing I could put in front of her which she could weigh. I'd be telling her a thing with no name that she has ever heard: that I take pieces of people when they come at me hard enough and mean it, and that there may be room in me for a tenth. What does a person say to that, at night, through a door?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:127:The sound of his own feet came back a beat late, like an echo off a wall. His hands had gone as cold as if he had held them in a spring. And the dark-backed one, which had heard nothing come and seen nothing come, shifted its grip to finish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:291:Cael turned as he fell. The rangy one was in the air over the stone, at the top of its leap, with its forelegs stretched and the flame's light on its teeth, and it was six feet from the nose of the bow. He did not see the bolt. He saw the animal fold in the middle of its spring, and come down on the western side of the stone like a thing dropped off a cart, and lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:305:He saw them only as shapes against the fire, and only for as long as it burned. They had been strung out down two hundred yards of hill behind a light, running to a declaration, and the declaration had gone over a stone and gone out; for he had let the flame die and the edge fall silent when the string slapped, and had shut his hand on all nine. What stood in front of them now was a fence of fire with their own road under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:147:She took the place nearest the door. She sat on the end of the bench with her boots drawn under her, as a person sits who may have to get up, and laid her two hands on the board where she could see them. Cael watched her do it. She ate at seven gates, round after round, whatever was put before her, and went out again in the morning; and she sat at this one like a woman who has sat at a great many tables and stayed at none of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:161:"Then you may have my bread," said Seln, "all of it, with my compliments," and sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:139:"That bone's a month old today. I said a month, on the mountain. It may run."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:153:He had that sum by heart, from the ninety-fifth day to the morning after a fortnight. Sixteen and one made seventeen, somewhere on a road that he had never seen; and she did not say where the one would fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:"So that's two boxes on two backs, and one left over, and a case that the quiet one won't put down." She counted it on her fingers. "And a girl who's to fall on her face sooner than put a hand out, on a road that's ice in the ruts. Who steadies her, when the pair of you have a hundredweight apiece?"

