editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:203:He took Hesk's book out of the satchel. He had to turn past a leaf to reach a clean one, and he knew that the square woman at the end of the bench had seen which leaf it was. He wetted the pencil, and wrote, with the firelight on the page and five people watching his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:237:Gault had signed a figure for him every week for two years, to the decimal, and had ended by writing that no instrument would have such a thing to measure again. Pike had one arm and a horn ink-pot, and had never owned an instrument in his life. Somebody had told him that some were lying at a place, and that a crew had gone to look; and he had put down that they were not there, and that the crew was. Cael thought that he would have liked to see the two men at one table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:309:The steading's woman cleared the board herself. She took six bowls on one arm, and left what was not hers to clear: a basket with three brown apples in it, and a book, and a scrap of bark. Cael drew Hesk's satchel up from under the bench to put the bark away with Pike's other lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:355:That was what brought the cold out on him. There was a leaf in the old volume of Hesk's book, the oldest unexplained thing that he owned, with a session's number at its head. Something had once come out of him in a practice yard that was none of his fragments, and it had come from under the breastbone, near the middle of him; a big hand had been laid on him afterward, and its owner had frowned and said that there was a quiet in there, under the noise. He had written the place down. He had turned that leaf over at every inventory since, and added nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:365:He got up, and took Hesk's book to the loft door, and wrote there by the stars, with the frost coming in round his feet and the pencil cold between his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:387:*Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — both ride east. Nineteen. Ten fragments. Hesk's package is on a ferry somewhere. The last one said mind the shape. I'm starting to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:215:*One. Two.* For ten counts after a lane, everything that he had answered him a beat late. He had written it so in Hesk's book, on a road, from a bout. It was a different thing to run in. His foot came down, and then he felt it come down. The flame leaned, and then he saw it lean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:63:Lira did not speak. She had been told once, on a mountain, to lie still and let it, and she lay still, and her right hand was the only part of her that told him anything. At first it had hold of the grass, a whole fistful, with the knuckles standing. A little later it left the grass and found the strap of Hesk's satchel, where that hung from his shoulder beside her, and took two turns of it round the wrist. By the time the oil stood halfway down the fourth finger the strap had gone slack, and the hand lay open on her own breast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:185:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, which was what an hour had left in it. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:67:He lay between two sacks with Hesk's book under his hand. Lira had brought it up the ladder at dusk inside her coat, and with it one sentence, said exactly as a healer had said it: that it was a true page, and that true was not the same as paid. He had written nothing in it since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:129:Cael carried one at a time. It was the first load that he had had on his back since a mountain, and the ribs took it without a word. He went up past his own blanket at each turn, and past Hesk's satchel on its nail, and set the sack down on a heap that had begun level with his knee and now stood as high as his chest; and by the tenth it had come to him that he was being walled into his own bedroom with other people's breakfast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:143:He knew that lag, for he had run up a ditch in it with eight hounds behind him. Whenever the heaviest thing in him had been used, everything else answered him a beat late for ten counts or twelve, and he had entered that in Hesk's book as a bill and learned to wait it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:245:Cael wrote one thing in Hesk's book before he went back to the sacks, since he wished it to be in his hand and dated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:5:A man asleep draws twelve to the minute. He had timed Brom against Hesk's clock once, on a road, for want of anything better to do, and had kept the figure. Twelve to the minute made sixty in five, and two hundred and forty in the twenty that she had named; and he lay on a scrubbed plank with a healer's hand over his breastbone and began at one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:75:He was back before she had moved from the sill, with Hesk's book held in both hands and a straw shut into it at the place. She did not take it at once. She looked at the hand that held it, as she looked at everything of hers first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:125:"I can read. It's on your leaf." She shut Hesk's book on the straw. "Say it to me again when it's cost you something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:141:She had brought Hesk's book with her from the sill, and she tapped its cover with one thick finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:183:He lay down. She put the horn cup into his hand, with water in it, and waited until he had drunk. Then she took Hesk's book back and sat down on the three-legged stool herself, for the first time since he had come into that room, with the book on her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:39:"Ephram was trying to beat me. He wasn't trying to hurt me, and the notice knew the difference; it said so, in two words that I'd never seen on one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:47:*Ephram fought you in earnest and the notice said non-hostile. This is earnest and it isn't a fight at all.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:277:He took the cream sheet out of Hesk's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:"That's how you find a handful. One at a time, for money." She nodded toward Hesk's satchel on its nail, where a broker's cream paper lay folded in a book. "Somebody paid a hundred marks to learn whether a crew would come out of the first, and I do not think that he has stopped wanting to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:147:He had not slept. He had come down the ladder with Hesk's book inside his shirt, and she was there already, a square shape against the white of the yard, filling a flask from the dipper. The mule stood at the trough with a blanket on her. There was a pannier on the coping with its strap buckled, and a coil of rope beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:43:Cael had the first watch. He sat with his back against a chock-stone and Hesk's book shut on his knee, and for some time he left it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:165:He had been a number in other people's books for as long as he could remember. A magister had given him one every week, to the decimal. Vell had kept one for him in a yard. Every one of those figures had been somebody's best account of how much of a thing he was; and here was a book-keeper with one arm, who had never owned an instrument, saying aloud in a room that his own book had a true line in it and was too small to hold what the line meant. Cael did not know what to do with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:185:Cael picked the paper up. It was the same weight as the other, which lay folded in Hesk's book; it had the same pricked margin, and one word standing alone at its head, with a stop after it. He held it so that the four of them could see, and read it out low, a stop at a time.

