editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:61:There had been four fingers of it in the glass when Brom struck the flint; he had noted that, as he noted everything. It went down by the breadth of one of them while a square woman knelt in the grass with her eyes shut. He held the flame where she had told him to hold it, and kept it from moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:185:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, which was what an hour had left in it. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:47:"I have set one point since the mountain, on a lamp-wick. It ought to be very nearly the whole." Her voice was small and dry. "Nine, as before, and the eight joins. I could hold them for a count of six and have something over. I was told not to spend it to the last copper where she was not, and she will be there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:173:The ground thumped like a drum. The grass lay over in a ring ten yards across, and the force of it came back up through his heels and his knees, as it came back off anything that would not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:185:*Yes*, he told that one too. He had never said so before. Along the knife's cutting side a line of white stood out a hair clear of the steel, as a tall exact person had once made one stand on a lath by saying that it did; and it rang. There was nothing for it to cut. It sang all the same, thin and high, like a wet finger going round the rim of a glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:7:"An Ember: Iron, third rank. Every place seen and none set, at no cost; and I said that I could not find the hand to reach with." She kept her eyes on the leaf. "An Iron Skin, Copper. He called it aloud and none came, and he said that the door was shut and that there was no next room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:11:"A Shadow, Bronze, who sought it and found that it was not there to be asked. A Wind: Iron, first rank, who asked for the whole of it and was given a step." Her voice had lost most of its hinge, and what was left of it creaked only on the long words. "And a Tide: Iron, fourth rank, with a count that stopped at four and nothing in her hands. That makes five Paths, and three tiers, and five people who had never before agreed about anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:133:It was a bow, and it was not his kind. His own leaned against the fence, as tall as he was. This was short and thick, an ash stock as long as her arm with a bow of horn and wood lashed across the nose of it, and an iron stirrup, and a long lever underneath. It was very old. Somebody had kept it oiled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:337:She could not answer him, and she looked at her right hand instead, which was sore across the palm from an ash stock, and which had put five of eight into a ring the size of a plate from forty paces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:167:She began instead with what other people called it. A carter's wife had given her *quiet ground*, across a yard, in three sentences. A one-armed man had said the same two words under an eave, and had told them where the sigil went to sleep. She had those entered as said. They were good words for a warning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:185:There was the carters' *sleep*. She liked that one least. A thing that sleeps has chosen to lie down, or has been tired; it dreams, and it wakes when it has rested. Nothing that she had measured gave her any of that. Her sigil had not grown drowsy. It had been in the room, and then the room had not been there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:79:There was one held note, thin and steady, like a nail that has been driven and has not quite done ringing. That one wanted the ladder's head. There was a higher one in his right arm, a wet finger going round the rim of a glass, which he had heard already on a hill with a knife in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:101:Brom did not swing. He set the butt of the haft against his own hip and lifted the other end, and the animal brought its throat down onto the ash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:129:"I told it. I've an ash pole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:224:There was no fright in it. It was the question that she would have put if a patient had gone white under her on the ash at Oxhollow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:371:He ruled a line under that, and wrote the inventory beneath, since a birthday was as good a day as any to count what he carried. There were ten confirmed. He set down the nine in their order, each with the Path that it stood beside and the person it had come from, and against the second of them he wrote *the Bronze from Ardenmere*, as he always did. He set down the tenth: Tide-adjacent, from Oryn; partial; Iron; himself, and whatever his hand was on. It was the first that did nothing in a fight, and the first that he had reached for knowing what he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:25:"There's a Wind's, and that one is Lira's. There's an Iron Skin's, which is Brom's, and an Ember's, which came from Karis. You know those three better than I do, since you've had your hands in them. Then a Pressure's, from a Bronze at Ardenmere, and a Compression's, from a fighter on a circuit. A Storm's, from a Gold. An Anchor's, from a hand whose face I never saw. A Blade's, which a friend gave me in a bout that he had asked for." He drew one slow breath. "And a Shadow's. That one I keep shut, for the sake of the person it came from, and I'll tell you its Path and nothing more. I used it once on that hill, for ten strides, in the lane. I looked first to see where your eyes were."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:33:"A light," said Brom. "Going up that hill the wrong way, with a noise on the end of it like a glass. And every dog on the slope turned its back on us and went after it. I'd a bow up and nothing whatever to shoot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:61:The cold had been at it. Where forty fires had burned when the roads were dry, he made out twelve, drawn in close about the great ash, and half the trodden grass had gone back to white. The ferry's cable stood across the water like a chalk line, with frost on it. The scow lay on the near bank. A brown gelding with one white foot was walking his circle in the capstan-shed's doorway with a blanket on his back, and Cael was glad to see that somebody had thought of the blanket. Both inns were smoking. There was a skin of ice along the eastern bank as wide as a cart, and beyond it the river ran dark and fast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:93:They went down into the meadow with the light going. The old place under the ash was empty, since a good many places were, and Brom unloaded there and rubbed the mule's back with a twist of hay while Seln walked once round the twelve fires and came back and said that there was nothing. A boy came for the standing-money. Karis gave him two coppers, which were too small a matter for her book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:119:Three old men sat beyond her, at boards of their own. Cael had seen them under the ash for a hundred days without ever learning what they did, three grey heads over a pair of dice. The dice lay on the wood between them now. The eldest had his hand half closed over them, and he neither threw nor gathered them up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:231:Seln gave it a count of sixty a side. Cael heard him do it, under his breath, exactly; and when the cake came off the stone it was pale gold on both faces, and risen, and there was not a black place on it anywhere.

